/*
  The four ranks in order, for the code that runs above the progression block.

  That block keeps its own copy in RANKS and this is not a second opinion: the
  file loads top to bottom, the stations are read hundreds of lines before the
  block registers itself on globalThis, and a station cannot borrow a name that
  does not exist yet. The two spellings have to agree or a seam gates on a rank
  nobody can hold, so the mining harness reads both out of this file and checks
  them word for word rather than leaving it to whoever edits one of them.
*/
const RANK_LADDER = ['novice', 'advanced', 'expert', 'master'];

const COOLDOWN_MS = 20000;

/*
  HOW LONG A SEAM IS DONE FOR, BY THE RANK THAT SEAM ASKS FOR.

  THE RULEBOOK DOES NOT SET THESE. classes.md says rank decides which source
  somebody may work and how much it gives, and says nothing about how often.
  These are invented, they are first numbers for the dev server, and they are
  here rather than inside mineOre so that changing the pace of every seam in
  the province is one edit in one place.

  WHY THE TWENTY SECONDS ABOVE COULD NOT STAY. COOLDOWN_MS was sized for the
  one salt quarry this server placed and the kiln beside it, and mineOre took
  it for all 581 seams because it was the number already there. Nothing else
  limits a seam: MineOreScript never runs on this server or on the client, for
  the two reasons written out over the scripted activators further down, so no
  vein is ever depleted and none ever reloots, and onActivate returns false for
  a seam, so the engine does nothing either. The cooldown is the entire supply.

  Measured on 20 September against the built table. A Master Miner takes 1 to 5
  off any master seam, three a strike on average. At twenty seconds that is 540
  ebony an hour off the one seam at 10e101:Skyrim.esm, and at the emerald seam
  28e95:Dragonborn.esm, where the ore is 600 gold, three a strike every twenty
  seconds is about 324,000 gold an hour off a single reference. Vanilla gives
  three strikes and then locks the vein for 30 in game days.

  The plant half of this work gave itself ten minutes, near enough the hour the
  engine reloots a FLOR in. An ore vein's own reloot is 30 in game days, which
  at the default timescale is a day and a half of real time, and a tester who
  saw a seam come back once a weekend would never see the ladder work at all.
  So these sit between the two, by what the seam is worth:

    novice    ten minutes, the same wait a picked plant gets. Iron is 2 gold
              and a novice takes 1 to 2, so one seam is about 18 gold an hour,
              which is the pace the plants already run at.
    advanced  half an hour. Corundum, orichalcum, quicksilver, silver, 20 to
              25 gold, and 310 of the 581 seams.
    expert    an hour. Moonstone, malachite, gold ore, 30 to 50 gold.
    master    four hours. The emerald seam above now gives about 1,800 gold
              every four hours instead of 324,000 an hour, and all 24 master
              seams worked in one circuit, including the six on Solstheim, come
              to about 15,000 gold before the first of them is ready again.

  The wait is kept per character and per reference in the same map the quarry
  and the plants use, and that map is memory: a server restart or a hot reload
  of this file clears every cooldown in it. That is true of the ten minutes on
  a plant as well, and it is the first thing to know before these numbers are
  argued about, because a four hour wait that a restart wipes is not four hours.
*/
const MINE_COOLDOWN_MS = {
  novice: 600000,
  advanced: 1800000,
  expert: 3600000,
  master: 14400000
};

const lastUse = new Map();   // "actorId:refrId" -> timestamp

/*
  WHAT TAKES A ROW BACK OUT OF lastUse.

  From the To Do List: "Any cache keyed by something unbounded gets an eviction
  rule: a size cap, an age sweep, or removal when the session ends." Every press
  that gives something adds a row here, one per character and per reference:
  the quarry, the kiln, every plant, every river, the hanging game and every
  carcass. Nothing ever took one out, so the map was bounded only by how long
  the server had been up. Measured on 24 September: about 140 bytes a row.

  A row older than the longest wait anybody asks about gives the answer a
  missing row gives, which is ready, because cooldownLeft reads a missing row
  as a stamp at time zero. So dropping those rows changes no answer anybody
  gets, and that is the whole rule. It is not removal when somebody leaves: a
  four hour wait on a master fishing spot has to outlast logging out and back.

  The longest wait is read off the tables rather than typed here. A number
  typed here that ever fell below one of them would hand a spot back early,
  and those four hours are the only limit on it. A new table of waits that
  stamps this map goes in longestWaitMs, and test-cooldown-sweep.js fails
  until it does.

  The sweep runs when a stamp takes the map past a mark, not on a timer. A
  stamp is the only way a row gets in, so this bounds the map without another
  interval to lose on a hot reload. After a sweep the mark moves to twice what
  is left, so a map full of rows that are all still waiting is walked once per
  doubling rather than on every press.
*/
const LAST_USE_SWEEP_FROM = 5000;
let lastUseSweepAt = LAST_USE_SWEEP_FROM;

function longestWaitMs() {
  let most = Math.max(COOLDOWN_MS, HARVEST_COOLDOWN_MS);
  const tables = [MINE_COOLDOWN_MS, FISH_TUNING.cooldownMs,
                  GAME_TUNING.cooldownMs, HUNT_TUNING.cooldownMs];
  for (const t of tables) {
    for (const k of Object.keys(t)) most = Math.max(most, Number(t[k]) || 0);
  }
  return most;
}

/** Drops every row older than the longest wait, and moves the mark. */
function forgetOldUses(now) {
  const keep = longestWaitMs();
  lastUse.forEach(function (at, key) {
    if (now - at > keep) lastUse.delete(key);
  });
  lastUseSweepAt = Math.max(LAST_USE_SWEEP_FROM, lastUse.size * 2);
}

/** Seconds still to wait, 0 if ready. Read only: a refused action must not
 *  start a cooldown, or walking up to a kiln without firewood locks you out.
 *  spanMs is how long this source stays worked out and defaults to the twenty
 *  seconds the stations use, because the quarry and the kiln were the only two
 *  callers when this was written. A seam passes its own off MINE_COOLDOWN_MS. */
function cooldownLeft(actorId, refrId, spanMs) {
  const span = spanMs || COOLDOWN_MS;
  const prev = lastUse.get(actorId + ':' + refrId) || 0;
  const left = span - (Date.now() - prev);
  return left > 0 ? Math.ceil(left / 1000) : 0;
}

/** Start the cooldown. Call this only once the yield has actually been given.
 *  Every row goes in through here, so the sweep above sees every one. */
function stampCooldown(actorId, refrId) {
  const now = Date.now();
  lastUse.set(actorId + ':' + refrId, now);
  if (lastUse.size >= lastUseSweepAt) {
    try { forgetOldUses(now); } catch (e) { lastUseSweepAt = lastUse.size * 2; }
  }
}

/**
 * A wait said the way a person says one.
 *
 * cooldownLeft answers in seconds and the quarry can say '14 more seconds'
 * because its wait is twenty. A master seam is four hours and '14400 more
 * seconds' is not a sentence anybody reads. Whole units, and the smaller one
 * only when it is not zero, which is the shape the farming block already says
 * a grow time in.
 */
function waitWord(seconds) {
  const s = Math.max(0, Math.ceil(seconds));
  if (s < 60) return s + (s === 1 ? ' second' : ' seconds');
  const m = Math.ceil(s / 60);
  if (m < 60) return m + (m === 1 ? ' minute' : ' minutes');
  const h = Math.floor(m / 60);
  const rest = m - h * 60;
  const said = h + (h === 1 ? ' hour' : ' hours');
  if (rest === 0) return said;
  return said + ' and ' + rest + (rest === 1 ? ' minute' : ' minutes');
}

function addItem(actorId, itemDesc, count) {
  const baseId = idOf(itemDesc);
  if (!baseId) return false;
  const inv = mp.get(actorId, 'inventory') || { entries: [] };
  const entries = inv.entries || [];
  let found = false;
  for (const e of entries) {
    if (e.baseId === baseId) { e.count += count; found = true; break; }
  }
  if (!found) entries.push({ baseId: baseId, count: count });
  mp.set(actorId, 'inventory', { entries: entries });
  return true;
}

function takeItem(actorId, itemDesc, count) {
  const baseId = idOf(itemDesc);
  if (!baseId) return false;
  const inv = mp.get(actorId, 'inventory') || { entries: [] };
  const entries = inv.entries || [];
  for (let i = 0; i < entries.length; i++) {
    if (entries[i].baseId !== baseId) continue;
    if (entries[i].count < count) return false;
    entries[i].count -= count;
    if (entries[i].count <= 0) entries.splice(i, 1);
    mp.set(actorId, 'inventory', { entries: entries });
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}

/*
  The same as takeItem, by form id rather than by descriptor.

  wearTool already knows the id of the tool in the pack, because it had to find
  out which of six pickaxes somebody is carrying. Turning that back into a
  descriptor to hand to takeItem would be a round trip through
  mp.getDescFromId for no reason, and one that can throw.
*/
function takeItemById(actorId, baseId, count) {
  if (!baseId) { return false; }
  let inv = null;
  try {
    inv = mp.get(actorId, 'inventory');
  } catch (e) {
    return false;
  }
  const entries = (inv && inv.entries) || [];
  for (let i = 0; i < entries.length; i++) {
    if (entries[i].baseId !== baseId) { continue; }
    if (entries[i].count < count) { return false; }
    entries[i].count -= count;
    if (entries[i].count <= 0) { entries.splice(i, 1); }
    mp.set(actorId, 'inventory', { entries: entries });
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}

function randInt(min, max) {
  return Math.floor(Math.random() * (max - min + 1)) + min;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// the class and profession gates
//
// classes.md: each class owns a verb that nobody else in the game has, and
// 'those are exclusive, not preferential'. Until 20 September nothing asked:
// canDo existed in the rules block and not one caller called it, so a miner
// picked flowers and a hunter worked a seam. Every place below that hands out
// a yield asks first, and says who can when the answer is no.
//
// The three wrappers go through the character block rather than the rules
// directly, because the character block is the only reader and writer of the
// state. When that block is missing, which means the file loaded broken, the
// gate is OPEN and says so in the log: a rules block that failed to register
// must not be the reason nobody in the world can pick a flower.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** null when this person may do that class verb, otherwise what to tell them. */
function classGate(actorId, verb) {
  const c = globalThis.__thornswoodCharacter;
  if (!c || !c.gate) {
    log('the character block is not loaded, so the', verb, 'gate is open');
    return null;
  }
  try {
    return c.gate(actorId, verb);
  } catch (e) {
    log('the', verb, 'gate threw, so it is open:', e);
    return null;
  }
}

/** The same for one half of a profession, at a rank. */
function halfGate(actorId, half, rank, refusal) {
  const c = globalThis.__thornswoodCharacter;
  if (!c || !c.gateHalf) {
    log('the character block is not loaded, so the', half, 'gate is open');
    return null;
  }
  try {
    return c.gateHalf(actorId, half, rank, refusal);
  } catch (e) {
    log('the', half, 'gate threw, so it is open:', e);
    return null;
  }
}

/**
 * A yield has been handed out: count the work, and hand back the line about a
 * new rank when one was reached. Anything going wrong here costs a count, not
 * somebody's salt, so it is swallowed.
 */
function creditWork(actorId, key) {
  /*
    Every piece of work is also a piece of tiredness.

    Blade: "When doing your skilled profession, it drains your Exhaustion
    faster. As you level up your skill it doesn't drain your exhaustion meter
    as fast." This is the one place every trade already passes through, so it
    is the one place that has to know: the seam, the chopping block, the kiln,
    the plot, the river and the hunt all call it, and anything added later
    gets the same treatment for nothing.

    Guarded and silent. A needs block that is not loaded yet, or a hot reload
    mid-tick, must not stop somebody's work being counted.
  */
  try {
    const needs = globalThis.__thornswoodNeeds;
    if (needs && needs.worked) {
      /*
        AND IT SAYS SO WHEN THE WORK ACTUALLY TELLS. worked() costs rest per
        swing now rather than nudging a rate, so it can cross a band, and a
        band crossing is the one thing worth interrupting somebody for. Every
        swing before that says nothing, which is the same rule the seam and
        the chopping block already follow.
      */
      const tiredLine = needs.worked(actorId);
      if (tiredLine) {
        try { notify(actorId, tiredLine); } catch (e) { }
        try { say(actorId, tiredLine); } catch (e) { }
      }
    }
  } catch (e) { }

  /*
    And work done with your party standing next to you counts for more.

    Blade's pitch had an experience bonus for playing together and Patrick
    said yes to it. This is the one place every trade already passes through,
    the same reason the tiredness above is here.

    PAID AS A WHOLE EXTRA PIECE, NOT AS A FRACTION. Work is counted in pieces:
    one seam, one log, one fish. There is no such thing as 1.15 of a log, and
    rolling a die for it would make the same work worth different amounts on
    different swings with nothing on the screen explaining why. So the bonus
    is banked per character and paid out as one extra piece when it reaches a
    whole one, which at fifteen percent is every seventh piece. Deterministic,
    and a sentence somebody can be told.

    IT IS ON BEING TOGETHER rather than on being in a party: partyNear asks
    whether anybody from your party is in the same cell. A bonus for being
    listed together is a checkbox everybody ticks once and never thinks about
    again.
  */
  let extra = false;
  try {
    const soc = globalThis.__thornswoodSocial;
    if (soc && soc.workBonus) {
      const over = soc.workBonus(actorId) - 1;
      if (over > 0) {
        PARTY_BANK[actorId] = (PARTY_BANK[actorId] || 0) + over;
        if (PARTY_BANK[actorId] >= 1) { PARTY_BANK[actorId] -= 1; extra = true; }
      }
    }
  } catch (e) { }

  const c = globalThis.__thornswoodCharacter;
  if (!c || !c.credit) { return null; }
  try {
    let line = c.credit(actorId, key);
    if (extra) {
      // The second piece's answer wins when it has one, because a rank raised
      // by the bonus piece is the thing worth saying.
      const more = c.credit(actorId, key);
      if (more) { line = more; }
    }
    return line;
  } catch (e) {
    log('could not count', key, 'work for', actorId.toString(16) + ':', e);
    return null;
  }
}

/* What each character is owed of the party bonus, in fractions of a piece.
   In memory: a party is a session, so its bonus is too. */
const PARTY_BANK = {};

/**
 * The rank somebody holds in one class, half or school, as a step: 0 for a
 * novice, 3 for a Master, and 0 when they hold nothing at all. The two
 * stations size their yield by this now rather than by a manual tier.
 */
function rankStep(actorId, key) {
  const c = globalThis.__thornswoodCharacter;
  if (!c || !c.rankOf) return 0;
  try {
    const at = RANK_LADDER.indexOf(c.rankOf(actorId, key));
    return at < 0 ? 0 : at;
  } catch (e) {
    log('could not read the', key, 'rank for', actorId.toString(16) + ':', e);
    return 0;
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// the three starting tools
//
// Patrick, 20 September: 'if you choose miner you should start with a pickaxe,
// unequipped is fine just in the inventory, the woodworker needs to start with
// an axe', and 'let's make it so to be able to farm you need a hoe so give
// farmers a hoe'.
//
// SKYRIM HAS NO HOE. Measured on 20 September across the whole load order, the
// masters and all 96 plugins after them: there is no record of any type that is
// a hoe. The only records that match the word at all are shoes. So the farmer's
// tool is a shovel, which is a real MISC record in Skyrim.esm and the thing
// actually leaning against the walls of the farms people will be standing in.
// The rulebook says so in its own words as well, on the public page, because a
// person who reads 'you need a hoe' and then cannot find one in any shop in the
// province has been told a lie by us and not by the game.
//
// The tool is IN THE PACK and nothing more. Nothing here equips anything: the
// server can write an inventory and cannot make somebody hold a weapon, and
// Patrick asked for unequipped anyway.
//
// Three verbs ask for a tool and no others. Gathering, hunting and fishing do
// not, because Patrick named three tools and three verbs, and inventing a
// skinning knife and a fishing rod to go with them would be a different design
// rather than a best guess at this one.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/*
  The tool each class or profession works with, keyed by the pick that earns it.

  ONE TOOL IS HANDED OVER AND SEVERAL COUNT AS HOLDING IT, and the second half
  of that was missing until 21 September. The first draft asked for one form id
  per tool, which is wrong about this game in a way that bites hardest on the
  farmer: Skyrim ships TWO shovels. Shovel01 and Shovel02 are separate MISC
  records in Skyrim.esm with the same model, the same name in the pack and no
  difference a person can see, and both of them sit side by side in
  LootMiningToolRandom05 and LootToolRandom05. Every record below was read out
  of the load order, and every count was made by walking every GRUP in every
  plugin and counting the placed references that point at it, on 21 September:

    weapPickaxe                   0x0E3C16   WEAP   Skyrim.esm      428 placed
    dunVolunruudPickaxe           0x1019D4   WEAP   Skyrim.esm        1  the Notched Pickaxe
    SSDRocksplinterPickaxe        0x06A707   WEAP   Skyrim.esm        0  Rocksplinter, handed out by a quest
    DLC2AncientNordPickaxe        0x0398E6   WEAP   Dragonborn.esm    1  the one that cuts stalhrim
    DLC2RR03NordPickaxe           0x0206F2   WEAP   Dragonborn.esm    1
    DLC2dunKolbjornRalisPickaxe   0x179C9    WEAP   Dragonborn.esm    0
    Axe01                         0x02F2F4   WEAP   Skyrim.esm      287 placed, the woodcutter's axe
    Shovel01                      0x0F5D05   MISC   Skyrim.esm      338 placed
    Shovel02                      0x0F5D06   MISC   Skyrim.esm      112 placed

  So 112 of the 450 shovels lying about the province are the one the first draft
  refused, and a farmer who lost the shovel they were handed, walked to a mill
  and picked up the shovel leaning on the wall had a one in four chance of being
  told they need a shovel while carrying one. Inside TOOL_AGAIN_HOURS of their
  last handout that is a hard refusal with no way out of it. Measured in the
  harness rather than worked out: toollens.js case G.

  The pickaxes are the same shape of problem with smaller numbers. Three of the
  six are quest items somebody may well be carrying and nothing else, and the
  Notched Pickaxe in particular is a thing people go and fetch on purpose. All
  six mine, so all six count.

  WHAT IS NOT IN THE LISTS. The war picks out of Immersive Weapons, IWSteelWar-
  Pick and the three beside it, are weapons shaped like a pick and are not mining
  tools; nothing in the game lets one work a seam and neither does this. And the
  woodcutter's axe has an accept list of one because there is only one: the whole
  load order, masters and all 96 plugins after them, was searched for another
  woodcutter's axe on 21 September and Axe01 is alone.

  The other farm tool in the masters is Pitchfork001, 0x0F5D07, also a MISC in
  Skyrim.esm. It is named here so that the next person to ask why the farmer
  carries a shovel can see that the other candidate was looked at and put aside:
  a pitchfork moves hay and a shovel turns ground, and turning ground is what
  the hoe was going to be for. Nothing hands a pitchfork out and nothing asks
  for one.

    give    the descriptor a handout adds. One of them, the common one.
    accept  every descriptor that counts as holding the tool, the given one
            first. holdsTool reads this and nothing else.
    name    what a sentence calls it. All three take 'a' in front of them.
    where   where to get another, said in the refusal, because 'you need a
            pickaxe' with no second sentence sends somebody to the Discord.
    broken  where to get another when the one they had just snapped, which is
            not the same sentence. Patrick, 21 September: a broken tool is
            replaced by "a smith making them", so the smith is named first and
            the world is named second. Both are true and the order is the
            point: the trade is meant to be the way back.
*/
const TOOLS = {
  miner: {
    give: 'e3c16:Skyrim.esm',
    accept: ['e3c16:Skyrim.esm', '1019d4:Skyrim.esm', '6a707:Skyrim.esm',
             '398e6:Dragonborn.esm', '206f2:Dragonborn.esm', '179c9:Dragonborn.esm'],
    name: 'pickaxe',
    where: 'A smith forges them. Nothing in the world hands one out any more: see the spoils block.',
    broken: 'A smith can forge you another. There is nowhere else to get one now.'
  },
  woodworker: {
    give: '2f2f4:Skyrim.esm',
    accept: ['2f2f4:Skyrim.esm'],
    name: 'woodcutter\'s axe',
    where: 'A smith forges them. There are no longer any lying at the mills: see the spoils block.',
    broken: 'A smith can forge you another. There is nowhere else to get one now.'
  },
  farmer: {
    give: 'f5d05:Skyrim.esm',
    accept: ['f5d05:Skyrim.esm', 'f5d06:Skyrim.esm'],
    name: 'shovel',
    where: 'Skyrim has no hoe, so a shovel is the tool, and a smith forges them. ' +
           'The ones that used to lean against every farm wall are gone: see the spoils block.',
    broken: 'A smith can forge you another. There is nowhere else to get one now.'
  }
};

/*
  HOW LONG BEFORE A LOST TOOL IS REPLACED. THE RULEBOOK DOES NOT SET THIS.

  classes.md does not mention a tool at all, so every number about one is
  invented. Twelve hours, which is the number TUNING.farming.seedAgainHours
  already runs on, for exactly the same reason: a tool handed out once at the
  pick and never again is a class somebody loses for good the first time they
  empty their pack into a chest, and every character made before this release
  has no tool at all because their pick is months of sessions behind them.

  It cannot be worked for profit. A pickaxe, a woodcutter's axe and a shovel are
  worth a few gold between them, the handout asks for a pack with none of that
  tool in it, and it happens at the seam, the kiln or the plot rather than
  anywhere a person would be standing anyway.
*/
const TOOL_AGAIN_HOURS = 12;

/*
  HOW MUCH WORK A TOOL HAS IN IT BEFORE IT BREAKS. NOT IN THE RULEBOOK.

  Patrick, 21 September: axes, pickaxes and shovels "can eventually break and
  you have to get a new one from a smith making them". So a tool stops being a
  thing somebody owns once and becomes stock, which somebody has to make.

  A hundred uses, measured against the ladder rather than picked out of the air.
  TUNING.workPerRank added up to 20 + 50 + 120 = 190 yields from novice to
  Master when this was written, so one tool carried somebody a little over half
  the way: two tools to the top, and a Master who keeps working keeps ordering.
  That was a standing order book for the smiths without being a chore.

  IT IS 100 + 250 + 600 = 950 NOW, changed on 21 September when a rank stopped
  meaning twenty seams and started meaning one. So the climb costs about ten
  tools rather than two, which is five times the order book and may well be too
  much of one. If the tool starts feeling like a toll rather than stock, this
  is the number to move and not the ladder.

  The warning comes at fifteen left. Far enough out to finish what you are doing
  and walk to a town, short enough to mean something.

  A use is counted after the work, never before it, so a refused swing costs
  nothing. Nothing here can take a tool somebody is not holding: if the pack
  cannot be read, or the tool is not in it, the swing wears nothing rather than
  breaking something at random.
*/
const TOOL_USES = 100;
const TOOL_WARN_AT = 15;

/*
  When each tool was last handed to this character, keyed the same way TOOLS is.
  On the actor, not in memory: a restart that forgot would hand every tool out
  again, and this is the only thing standing between the way back and a tap.
*/
const TOOL_PROPERTY = 'thornswoodTools';

/** The tool a class or profession works with, or null when it needs none. */
function toolFor(key) {
  return Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(TOOLS, key) ? TOOLS[key] : null;
}

/*
  Is that tool in the pack.

  EVERY DESCRIPTOR IN tool.accept, not just the one a handout gives. Skyrim has
  two shovels and six pickaxes and a person cannot tell one shovel from the
  other, so asking for one form id would refuse somebody holding the tool. See
  the TOOLS block for the six counts that were walked to settle that.

  TWO READS ANSWER YES RATHER THAN NO, and both are deliberate. A tool none of
  whose descriptors resolve is a plugin missing from the load order, and an
  inventory read that throws is the server failing to answer a question about
  somebody who is plainly standing there. Neither of those is the person's
  fault, and a build fault must not be the reason nobody in the world can work a
  seam. The same choice classGate makes when the character block is missing.

  A descriptor that resolves to 0 on its own is passed over rather than treated
  as the whole tool missing. Dragonborn.esm is in this load order and is not in
  every load order, and a miner holding the plain pickaxe must not be refused
  because the Solstheim one could not be looked up.
*/
function holdsTool(actorId, tool) {
  const wanted = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < tool.accept.length; i++) {
    const baseId = idOf(tool.accept[i]);
    if (baseId) { wanted.push(baseId); }
  }
  if (!wanted.length) {
    log('no', tool.name, 'in this load order, so the tool is not asked for');
    return true;
  }
  let inv = null;
  try {
    inv = mp.get(actorId, 'inventory');
  } catch (e) {
    log('could not read the pack of', actorId.toString(16), 'so the tool is not asked for:', e);
    return true;
  }
  const entries = (inv && inv.entries) || [];
  for (let i = 0; i < entries.length; i++) {
    const e = entries[i];
    if (!e || !(e.count > 0)) { continue; }
    for (let j = 0; j < wanted.length; j++) {
      if (e.baseId === wanted[j]) { return true; }
    }
  }
  return false;
}

/** When each tool was last handed over. Always an object, never a throw. */
function toolStamps(actorId) {
  let v = null;
  try {
    v = mp.get(actorId, TOOL_PROPERTY);
  } catch (e) {
    v = null;
  }
  if (!v || typeof v !== 'object') { v = {}; }
  return v;
}

/**
 * The tool into the pack, and the moment written down. The tool, or null when
 * nothing reached them. Called by both doors below and by neither directly.
 */
function handTool(actorId, key) {
  const tool = toolFor(key);
  if (!tool) { return null; }
  if (!addItem(actorId, tool.give, 1)) {
    log('there is no', tool.name, 'in this load order, so', actorId.toString(16),
        'was handed nothing');
    return null;
  }
  const stamps = toolStamps(actorId);
  stamps[key] = Date.now();
  try {
    mp.set(actorId, TOOL_PROPERTY, stamps);
  } catch (e) {
    // The tool is in the pack and the moment is not written down, so the way
    // back below will hand another one over. That is the harmless direction.
    log('could not record the', key, 'tool handout for', actorId.toString(16) + ':', e);
  }
  log(actorId.toString(16), 'was handed a', tool.name, 'for', key);
  return tool;
}

/** The line to say when a pick hands the tool over, or null. */
function giveTool(actorId, key) {
  const tool = handTool(actorId, key);
  if (!tool) { return null; }
  return 'You are handed a ' + tool.name + '. Keep it in your pack: the work needs it there.';
}

/*
  THE WAY BACK, and it is the same way back the farmer's seed has.

  offerSeed in the farming block exists because seed used to be handed out at
  exactly one moment, the pick, so a Farmer who lost their crops was a Farmer who
  could never farm again and every Farmer who picked the class on an earlier
  build was born in that state. A tool that is required to work is the same shape
  of trap and it is worse, because nobody who exists today has one.

  So the tool is asked for rather than given: this runs at the seam, the kiln or
  the plot, where somebody is standing saying they want to do the work, and never
  from a read. Two conditions, and the pack one is the real one: no tool of that
  kind in the pack at all, and TOOL_AGAIN_HOURS since the last one.
*/
function offerTool(actorId, key) {
  const stamps = toolStamps(actorId);
  const last = Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(stamps, key)
    ? (Number(stamps[key]) || 0) : 0;
  if (last && TOOL_AGAIN_HOURS > 0 &&
      (Date.now() - last) < TOOL_AGAIN_HOURS * 3600000) {
    return null;
  }
  const tool = handTool(actorId, key);
  if (!tool) { return null; }
  return 'You had no ' + tool.name + ', so here is one. Do that again now.';
}

/**
 * null when this person is holding the tool the work needs, otherwise the
 * sentence to say: the one handing a replacement over, or the refusal.
 *
 * ALWAYS AFTER THE CLASS OR HALF GATE AND BEFORE THE COOLDOWN. Somebody who is
 * not a miner should be told they are not a miner rather than handed a pickaxe,
 * and a refusal that started a cooldown would tell them the wrong thing about
 * why nothing happened. That is the same ordering mineOre, harvest() and the
 * farming block already settled on.
 */
/*
  WHICH tool of the accepted ones is in this pack, as a form id.

  holdsTool already walks this list to answer yes or no; the client needs the
  id itself to put the thing in somebody's hands. Six pickaxes are accepted
  and the one they own is the one that should be drawn, not a stand-in.
  Nothing carried answers 0, which the client reads as no tool and leaves
  their hands alone.
*/
function toolIdIn(actorId, key) {
  const tool = toolFor(key);
  if (!tool) { return 0; }
  const wanted = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < tool.accept.length; i++) {
    const baseId = idOf(tool.accept[i]);
    if (baseId) { wanted.push(baseId); }
  }
  if (!wanted.length) { return 0; }
  let inv = null;
  try { inv = mp.get(actorId, 'inventory'); } catch (e) { return 0; }
  const entries = (inv && inv.entries) || [];
  for (let i = 0; i < entries.length; i++) {
    const e = entries[i];
    if (!e || !(e.count > 0)) { continue; }
    for (let j = 0; j < wanted.length; j++) {
      if (e.baseId === wanted[j]) { return e.baseId; }
    }
  }
  return 0;
}

function toolGate(actorId, key) {
  const tool = toolFor(key);
  if (!tool) { return null; }
  if (holdsTool(actorId, tool)) { return null; }
  /*
    NO TOOL, NO WORK, AND NOTHING IS HANDED OVER.

    Patrick, 21 September, after playing it: "Don't let p[eople chop wood, mine,
    or farm without a pickaxe, shovel, or woodcutters axe."

    This used to call offerTool here, which gave anybody with an empty pack a
    free tool once every twelve hours and told them to try again. So nobody was
    ever actually refused for want of one: the gate read as a gate and behaved
    as a vending machine, and the tool-breaking system it was written beside had
    nothing to bite on, because a snapped pickaxe was replaced for free by the
    thing that was supposed to make you go and buy another.

    A tool comes from a smith now, which is the whole point of the handle and
    the recipes that need one. offerTool and handTool are left where they are:
    they are how the FIRST tool of a starting profession is placed, and taking
    them out of this gate is what stops them being an endless supply.
  */
  return 'You need a ' + tool.name + ' in your pack for that. ' + tool.where;
}

/*
  The form id of the tool of this kind actually in the pack, or 0.

  holdsTool answers yes or no and that was enough while nothing ever took one
  away. Breaking one has to know WHICH, because a miner may be carrying any of
  six pickaxes and the Notched Pickaxe off the Throat of the World is not the
  one to snap because it happens to be first in the accept list. So this returns
  the one they are holding, and when they are holding more than one it returns
  the first in accept order, which puts the plain handout ahead of the quest
  items in every one of the three lists.
*/
function heldToolId(actorId, tool) {
  let inv = null;
  try {
    inv = mp.get(actorId, 'inventory');
  } catch (e) {
    return 0;
  }
  const entries = (inv && inv.entries) || [];
  for (let i = 0; i < tool.accept.length; i++) {
    const baseId = idOf(tool.accept[i]);
    if (!baseId) { continue; }
    for (let j = 0; j < entries.length; j++) {
      const e = entries[j];
      if (e && e.count > 0 && e.baseId === baseId) { return baseId; }
    }
  }
  return 0;
}

/*
  One use off the tool. Called AFTER the work is done and the yield is handed
  over, never before, so a refused swing costs nothing.

  Returns the sentence to say, or null when there is nothing worth saying. Three
  answers: silence, the warning near the end, and the break.

  WHAT A BREAK DOES. The tool comes out of the pack and the handout clock is
  set to now, which is the whole of the balance. TOOL_AGAIN_HOURS is twelve, so
  for twelve hours the free replacement at the seam is not coming and the way
  back is a smith, which is what Patrick asked for. After twelve hours the old
  safety net is there again, because a tool that is required to work must never
  be the reason somebody's class is over: that is the same trap offerTool and
  offerSeed were both written to close, and breaking one must not reopen it.

  The count is kept beside the handout stamps under a 'wear:' key rather than in
  a second property. offerTool reads stamps[key] and nothing else, so a
  'wear:miner' sitting next to 'miner' is invisible to it, every character who
  already has stamps keeps them, and there is no migration to get wrong.
*/
function wearTool(actorId, key) {
  const tool = toolFor(key);
  if (!tool) { return null; }
  const baseId = heldToolId(actorId, tool);
  if (!baseId) { return null; }

  const stamps = toolStamps(actorId);
  const wearKey = 'wear:' + key;
  const used = (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(stamps, wearKey)
    ? (Number(stamps[wearKey]) || 0) : 0) + 1;

  if (used < TOOL_USES) {
    stamps[wearKey] = used;
    try {
      mp.set(actorId, TOOL_PROPERTY, stamps);
    } catch (e) {
      // The wear is not written down, so this swing was free. Harmless: the
      // tool lasts longer than it should rather than breaking in their hand.
      log('could not record', key, 'tool wear for', actorId.toString(16) + ':', e);
      return null;
    }
    const left = TOOL_USES - used;
    if (left === TOOL_WARN_AT) {
      return 'Your ' + tool.name + ' is worn thin. It has a little work left in it.';
    }
    return null;
  }

  // Worn out. Take it, forget the count, and hold the free replacement off.
  if (!takeItemById(actorId, baseId, 1)) {
    // It could not be taken, so it is not broken. Counting it again next swing
    // is the honest answer; snapping a tool that is still in the pack is not.
    log('could not take the worn', tool.name, 'from', actorId.toString(16));
    return null;
  }
  stamps[wearKey] = 0;
  stamps[key] = Date.now();
  try {
    mp.set(actorId, TOOL_PROPERTY, stamps);
  } catch (e) {
    log('could not record the broken', key, 'tool for', actorId.toString(16) + ':', e);
  }
  log(actorId.toString(16), 'broke a', tool.name, 'after', TOOL_USES, 'uses');
  return 'Your ' + tool.name + ' breaks. ' + tool.broken;
}

/**
 * Salt quarry: the miner's verb, and the miner's RANK sizes the yield.
 *
 * The mining manual used to gate this and set the yield. That was a second
 * progression system beside the rulebook's own, and classes.md has only one:
 * the class owns the verb and the rank comes from doing the work. So the
 * manual no longer stands between anybody and a seam, and how much salt comes
 * away follows the Miner rank that the seam itself is raising.
 */
function mineSalt(actorId, refrId) {
  // The class first, and before the cooldown: somebody who is not a miner is
  // never going to work this seam, and a refusal that starts a cooldown is
  // telling them the wrong thing about why.
  const mine = classGate(actorId, 'mine');
  if (mine) return mine;

  // The pickaxe, after the class and before the cooldown. See toolGate.
  const tool = toolGate(actorId, 'miner');
  if (tool) return tool;

  const step = rankStep(actorId, 'miner');

  /*
    A press inside a swing this person is already taking, ignored in silence.
    The twenty second cooldown below would refuse it anyway, but it would
    refuse it with 'the seam needs 19 more seconds', and an early click is not
    a person who came back too soon. See swinging().
  */
  if (swinging(actorId)) { return ''; }

  const wait = cooldownLeft(actorId, refrId);
  if (wait > 0) return 'The seam needs ' + wait + ' more seconds to be worth striking.';

  /*
    The pick goes up on the press and the salt comes away when it lands, the
    same ordering mineOre settled on and for the same reason Patrick gave:
    "you dont get the ore as it hits the vein". The cooldown is stamped on the
    press, with the seam rather than with the salt, so a click during the
    swing cannot buy a second strike at it.
  */
  stampCooldown(actorId, refrId);
  swing(actorId, SWING_MINE, function () {
    const yielded = randInt(1, 2 + step);
    if (!addItem(actorId, ITEMS.saltPile, yielded)) {
      notify(actorId, 'The seam yields nothing you can carry.');
      say(actorId, 'The seam yields nothing you can carry.');
      return;
    }
    let extra = '';
    if (step >= 2 && Math.random() < 0.12 * (step - 1)) {
      const rare = [ITEMS.fireSalts, ITEMS.frostSalts, ITEMS.voidSalts][randInt(0, 2)];
      addItem(actorId, rare, 1);
      extra = ' Something rarer came away with it.';
    }
    const rank = creditWork(actorId, 'mine');
    const worn = wearTool(actorId, 'miner');
    const saying = 'You work ' + yielded + ' salt from the seam.' + extra +
                   (rank ? ' ' + rank : '') + (worn ? ' ' + worn : '');
    notify(actorId, saying);
    say(actorId, saying);
  });
  return '';
}

/**
 * A rank named in a sentence, with the article that belongs in front of it:
 * 'a Novice', 'an Expert'. The farming block has the same three lines inside
 * its closure and cannot be reached from here, so a refusal at a seam and a
 * refusal at a plot read the same way because both were written to, not
 * because one calls the other.
 */
function rankWord(rank) {
  const n = String(rank || '').charAt(0).toUpperCase() + String(rank || '').slice(1);
  return (/^[AEIOU]/.test(n) ? 'an ' : 'a ') + n;
}

/** True when this base is one of the 581 seams in the generated table above.
 *  Never a bare lookup, for the reason own() in the progression block was
 *  written: the descriptor names a record in the world, and '__proto__' finds
 *  something on Object.prototype that a truth test cannot tell from a seam. */
function isVein(baseDesc) {
  return Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(ORES_BY_BASE, baseDesc);
}

/**
 * Every other seam in Skyrim: the miner's verb again, and the table sizes the
 * yield instead of the rank arithmetic the quarry uses.
 *
 * The salt quarry above is one base this server put in the world. These are
 * the 581 the game already had, and until now not one of them answered.
 * Pressing an iron vein did nothing at all: mp.onActivate had no branch for
 * it, and the engine's own mining is MineOreScript, a Papyrus script, which
 * for the two reasons set out at the scripted activators further down never
 * runs on this server or on the client. So a Miner could work the one quarry
 * and walked past every seam in the province.
 *
 * The shape is mineSalt's, line for line, and the four differences are the
 * table's doing. The yield is the pair for the miner's rank instead of
 * randInt(1, 2 + step). A pair of zeroes is refused by name rather than handed
 * a smaller pile, so a Novice at an ebony seam is told what it takes rather
 * than left to work out why nothing came off it. There is no rare-drop roll:
 * the quarry has one because salt is the only thing a salt seam can give, and
 * a seam here already gives what it is made of. And the wait between two
 * strikes is the seam's own, off MINE_COOLDOWN_MS, ten minutes at an iron vein
 * and four hours at a gem geode, where the quarry's is a flat twenty seconds.
 * That last one is not a refinement: it is the only limit on 581 seams the
 * engine never depletes, and the case for the numbers is written over the
 * table itself.
 */

/*
  ============================ a seam holds twenty, and gives one at a time ===

  Patrick, 21 September, after playing it: "make each quarry have up to 20 ore
  and not skip the animation and the same with chopping where you get an ore
  each time you hit it you know not all at omce so you can stay for a little
  and get a little or stay andd get all 20", with ten minutes to come back for
  iron, gold, corundum and silver and thirty for moonstone, quicksilver,
  orichalcum, ebony and malachite.

  WHAT THIS REPLACES. A seam handed over its whole yield on one press and then
  refused everybody for between ten minutes and four hours. So a seam was a
  vending machine with a long queue: one strike, a pile of ore, and nothing to
  come back to. A deposit is a pile of twenty that anybody may work down one
  swing at a time, and it is the same seam for whoever is standing there, so
  two miners share a vein rather than each having their own.

  AND THE ANIMATION IS NOT CANCELLED. Every branch of the activate chain ends
  at `return false`, which blocks the engine's own handover, and for a seam
  that is what threw the mining animation away. Mining answers ALLOW now, which
  says "I have handled the yield, let the press go on being a press", and the
  chain returns undefined for it.

  THE POOL LIVES ON THE REFERENCE, not on the person, because a seam is worked
  out for everybody or for nobody. It is written with the reference's own
  property, the same way the spoils block marks a container it has emptied.
*/
/*
  "I HAVE HANDLED THIS, LET THE PRESS GO ON BEING A PRESS."

  Every branch of the activate chain ends at `return false`, which blocks the
  engine's own handover. For a container or a plant that is exactly right: the
  server has already given what there was to give. For a SEAM it threw away the
  mining animation, which is the thing Patrick asked for back.

  A branch that answers this has taken care of the yield and wants the press to
  carry on, so the chain returns undefined for it and Skyrim swings the pick.
*/
const ALLOW = { thornswoodAllow: true };

const DEPOSIT_SIZE = 20;
const SWING_PROPERTY = 'thornswoodSwing';

/*
  ===================== PUT YOUR CLOTHES ON =================================

  Patrick, 23 September, and it has been reported since the 21st: "Still
  spawned in with no clothes on".

  THE SERVER CANNOT DRESS ANYBODY, and that is the whole of it. giveShift
  puts a Roughspun Tunic and Footwraps in the pack with worn set on the
  INVENTORY entry, which is the only thing a gamemode can write: the
  equipment record is a separate field and its property binding has a Get
  and no Set. So the items arrive and nothing on the client ever equips
  them. The client dresses itself from the EQUIPMENT record, which for a
  new character is empty, so the character stands there in their skin with
  a tunic in the pack.

  That is one half of why clothes never changed the temperature either: the
  equipment update the client sends is what fills the server's record, and a
  client that was never asked to wear anything sends nothing to record.

  So the server asks, the same way it asks for a swing: an updateOwner
  script, edge triggered on a stamp through ctx.state, that equips the base
  ids it is given. Silently and without preventing removal, so nothing here
  can trap anything on anybody, and they can take it off the moment they
  find something better.
*/
const WEAR_PROPERTY = 'thornswoodWear';

/*
  ==================== PUT THAT PLANT BACK ================================

  Patrick, 23 September: "all of the harvestable ones correctly don't give
  it to you if you aren't the right class but it removes it so someone who
  is the right class has to wait for it to grow back it shouldn't be
  harvested and removed from your inventory it should just not be harvested
  at all".

  He is right, and refusing the press is not enough on its own. Picking a
  plant happens on the machine drawing the world: by the time onActivate
  answers, that client has already played the animation, emptied the bush and
  put the flower in its own pack. Returning false stops the SERVER handing
  anything over, and the inventory sync then takes the flower back, so the
  person gets nothing, which is right, and the bush stays empty for everybody
  until it regrows, which is wrong. A gatherer walking up behind them finds a
  stripped plant that nobody got anything from.

  So the refusal says so to the client, which is the only place that can undo
  it: ObjectReference.setHarvested(false) is the engine's own way to put a
  picked plant back, and it is the exact opposite of what the press just did.
*/
const PUTBACK_PROPERTY = 'thornswoodPutBack';

function putBackScript() {
  return [
    '(function(){',
    '  try {',
    '    var v = ctx.value;',
    '    if (!v || !v.at || !v.ref) { return; }',
    '    var st = ctx.state;',
    '    if (!st) { return; }',
    '    if (st.thornswoodPutBackAt === v.at) { return; }',
    '    st.thornswoodPutBackAt = v.at;',
    '    var sp = ctx.sp;',
    '    var ref = sp.ObjectReference.from(sp.Game.getFormEx(v.ref));',
    '    if (!ref) { return; }',
    // Only when it actually went. A press that was refused before the engine
    // took anything leaves nothing to put back, and setting it twice would
    // be a plant that never empties.
    '    if (!ref.isHarvested()) { return; }',
    '    ref.setHarvested(false);',
    '  } catch (e) { }',
    '})();'
  ].join('\n');
}

/** True when the toast means produce was handed over; leave the flora empty. */
function floraHanded(message) {
  return typeof message === 'string' && /^(You pick |You take )/.test(message);
}

/** Put back whatever that press just picked. See PUTBACK_PROPERTY. */
function putBack(actorId, refrId) {
  if (!actorId || !refrId) { return; }
  try { mp.set(actorId, PUTBACK_PROPERTY, { ref: refrId, at: Date.now() }); } catch (e) { return; }
  // Cleared behind it, so nothing replays at the next login and un-picks a
  // plant somebody has since taken properly.
  setTimeout(function () {
    try { mp.set(actorId, PUTBACK_PROPERTY, { ref: 0, at: 0 }); } catch (e) { }
  }, 4000);
}

function wearScript() {
  return [
    '(function(){',
    '  try {',
    '    var v = ctx.value;',
    '    if (!v || !v.at || !v.ids || !v.ids.length) { return; }',
    '    var st = ctx.state;',
    '    if (!st) { return; }',
    '    if (st.thornswoodWearAt === v.at) { return; }',
    '    st.thornswoodWearAt = v.at;',
    '    var sp = ctx.sp;',
    '    var pc = sp.Game.getPlayer();',
    '    if (!pc) { return; }',
    '    for (var i = 0; i < v.ids.length; i++) {',
    '      try {',
    '        var it = sp.Form.from(sp.Game.getFormEx(v.ids[i]));',
    '        if (it) { pc.equipItem(it, false, true); }',
    '      } catch (e) { }',
    '    }',
    '    try {',
    '      var ac = sp.Actor.from(pc);',
    '      if (ac) { ac.queueNiNodeUpdate(); }',
    '    } catch (e) { }',
    '  } catch (e) { }',
    '})();'
  ].join('\n');
}

/*
  PUT BACK ON WHATEVER THE SERVER BELIEVES IS WORN.

  Dressing at creation is not enough, and that is its own entry on the list:
  "You come back undressed from a session you left dressed". The server's
  inventory keeps the worn flags across a session; the client rebuilds
  itself from the equipment record, and anything that empties or misses that
  record leaves somebody standing in their skin with a full pack.

  So on arrival, every worn thing in the server's own inventory is sent back
  as an ask. Somebody already wearing them is asked to equip what they are
  already wearing, which the engine does nothing with, so the cost of being
  wrong here is nothing at all.

  ONLY WHAT THE SERVER ALREADY HAS. Nothing is granted and nothing is
  invented: an item the server has no record of cannot be worn by this, the
  same way it cannot be counted for warmth.
*/
/*
  ================== AND IT FIXES ITSELF WHERE YOU STAND =================

  Patrick, 23 September, on being told to relog to see the clothes come
  back: "this a shitty solutiuon it shopuld do it automatically". He is
  right. Dressing somebody at the door only helps the person who walks
  through it afterwards, and anybody already standing in the world in their
  skin stays that way until they quit the game, which is a workaround
  wearing a fix's clothes.

  So the server watches instead. It can READ the equipment record even
  though it cannot write one, so it can see the difference between what
  somebody is wearing and what their pack says they are wearing, and that
  difference is the whole bug.

  ONLY WHEN THEY ARE WEARING NOTHING AT ALL, which is the line between
  fixing this and fighting the person. Taking your boots off is a decision.
  Standing in an inn with a full pack and no clothes on is not a decision
  anybody made, and it is the only state this acts on: an empty equipment
  record beside a pack that says something is worn.

  NOTHING IS ASKED OF THE PERSON. There is no prompt and nothing appears on
  screen: the server tells the client to equip what the pack already says is
  worn, silently, and it is on. If you left your clothes on, your clothes
  are on.

  AND IT GIVES UP. Three goes, a quarter of a minute apart, and then it
  stops for the session and says so once. A client that will not equip what
  it is handed is a different fault, and a server that keeps at it forever
  is a server nobody can read the log of.
*/
/*
  ONCE, AND NOT AGAIN FOR TEN MINUTES.

  This was three goes a quarter of a minute apart, and Patrick found what
  that costs the moment the client would not take the clothes: "I heard the
  noise of things equipping and the right hand side of my screen showed
  things going into my inventory that already were". An equip ask the client
  cannot satisfy is an ask that will not be satisfied by being repeated, and
  repeating it is noise on somebody's screen while they are trying to play.

  So it is one ask, and then nothing for ten minutes. The arrival ask is
  still there for the case this exists for, and if the client genuinely
  cannot equip what the server believes it is wearing, that is a different
  fault and a quiet log line is the right way to find it rather than an
  equip storm.
*/
const DRESS_EVERY = 600000;   // ten minutes between looks
const DRESS_TRIES = 1;        // and one ask, then it stops for the session
const dressAt = {};           // actorId -> when they were last asked
const dressTried = {};        // actorId -> how many times

/** What the client actually has on, by base id, or null when it cannot be read. */
function wornNow(actorId) {
  let eq = null;
  try { eq = mp.get(actorId, 'equipment'); } catch (e) { return null; }
  if (!eq) { return null; }
  const entries = (eq.inv && eq.inv.entries) || eq.entries || [];
  const out = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < entries.length; i++) {
    const e = entries[i];
    if (e && e.worn && e.baseId) { out.push(e.baseId); }
  }
  return out;
}

/*
  Somebody standing there with nothing on and clothes in the pack, put right.
  Answers what it did, for the log.
*/
function dressIfBare(actorId) {
  const now = Date.now();
  if (dressAt[actorId] && now - dressAt[actorId] < DRESS_EVERY) { return ''; }
  if ((dressTried[actorId] || 0) >= DRESS_TRIES) { return ''; }

  const on = wornNow(actorId);
  // Unreadable is not empty. A record that cannot be read is left alone.
  if (on === null) { return ''; }
  if (on.length) {
    // Dressed. Whatever went wrong earlier is over, so the counter goes back.
    delete dressTried[actorId];
    return '';
  }

  dressAt[actorId] = now;
  const put = wearWhatIsWorn(actorId);
  if (!put) { return ''; }
  dressTried[actorId] = (dressTried[actorId] || 0) + 1;
  if (dressTried[actorId] >= DRESS_TRIES) {
    return 'put ' + put + ' thing(s) back on for the last time this session; ' +
           'the client is not taking them';
  }
  return 'wearing nothing with ' + put + ' worn thing(s) in the pack, put back on';
}

function wearWhatIsWorn(actorId) {
  let inv = null;
  try { inv = mp.get(actorId, 'inventory'); } catch (e) { return 0; }
  const entries = (inv && inv.entries) || [];
  const ids = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < entries.length; i++) {
    const e = entries[i];
    if (e && e.worn && e.baseId) { ids.push(e.baseId); }
  }
  if (!ids.length) { return 0; }
  wearThese(actorId, ids);
  return ids.length;
}

/*
  Ask somebody to put something on, and take the ask back afterwards.

  Cleared for the same reason the swing is: a property left on a character is
  stored, handed to the owner again at the next login, and played again by a
  client that has never seen it. A stale one would re-dress somebody in
  clothes they had deliberately taken off.
*/
function wearThese(actorId, baseIds) {
  const ids = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < (baseIds || []).length; i++) {
    const id = Number(baseIds[i]) || 0;
    if (id) { ids.push(id); }
  }
  if (!ids.length) { return; }
  try { mp.set(actorId, WEAR_PROPERTY, { ids: ids, at: Date.now() }); } catch (e) { return; }
  setTimeout(function () {
    try { mp.set(actorId, WEAR_PROPERTY, { ids: [], at: 0 }); } catch (e) { }
  }, 4000);
}

/*
  THE ONE WAY TO PUT SOMETHING IN FRONT OF SOMEBODY'S EYES.

  Patrick, 22 September, about the restart countdown: "I need it to do an
  onscreen warninjg and not just in chat at one minute and at 30 seoconds
  before you get kciked from the server incase people miss the message in
  chat", and about the meters: "There is no indicator for the hunger food or
  exhasution on screen".

  Both are the same missing thing. Everything this server says lands in the
  chat overlay, which is a small panel somebody may have scrolled away from or
  simply not be looking at, and the two messages that must not be missed are
  the one that ends in being thrown out and the one that says why you have
  started missing your swings.

  IT DRAWS ITSELF, WITH NO PAGE TO DEPEND ON. The script below builds its own
  element in the overlay document. It does not call into thornswood-chat or
  any other page, so it works on a client whose overlay is from before this
  existed, and it needs no Ship: an updateOwner script travels with the
  gamemode, so a deploy is enough to put a HUD on everybody's screen.

  AND Debug.notification BESIDE IT, which is Skyrim's own corner message and
  needs no browser at all. If the overlay never mounted, that one still lands.
*/
const ALERT_PROPERTY = 'thornswoodAlert';

/*
  How long a banner stays up before it fades, by how much it matters.
  A countdown warning outlives the next one so the screen is never empty
  between 60 and 30.
*/
const ALERT_MS = 9000;

function alertScript() {
  return [
    '(function(){',
    '  try {',
    '    var v = ctx.value;',
    '    if (!v || !v.text || !v.at) { return; }',
    // updateOwner runs EVERY FRAME. See the note over SWING_PROPERTY.
    '    var st = ctx.state;',
    '    if (!st) { return; }',
    '    if (st.thornswoodAlertAt === v.at) { return; }',
    '    st.thornswoodAlertAt = v.at;',
    '    var sp = ctx.sp;',
    // Skyrim's own corner message first, because it needs no browser.
    '    try { sp.Debug.notification(String(v.text)); } catch (e) { }',
    /*
      And the banner. Built as one string here and handed to the overlay, so
      the page is never asked to have a function for it. JSON.stringify does
      the quoting, which is why nothing in the text has to be escaped by hand.
    */
    '    var body = JSON.stringify(String(v.text));',
    '    var ms = Number(v.ms) || 9000;',
    '    var tone = (v.tone === "bad") ? "#e0645a" : "#d9ab52";',
    '    var src = "(function(){try{" +',
    '      "var d=document.getElementById(\'ts-alert\');" +',
    '      "if(!d){d=document.createElement(\'div\');d.id=\'ts-alert\';" +',
    /*
      A THIRD OF THE WAY DOWN, NOT A NINTH.

      Patrick, 23 September: "The on screen indicator is to high up on the
      screen". It sat at 11%, which on a wide screen is level with the compass
      and above where anybody is actually looking. 30% puts it in the upper
      part of the eye line, clear of the compass at the top and clear of the
      crosshair and the activate prompt in the middle.

      max-width is here for the same message: the wording below is a whole
      sentence now, and without a width a long one stretches to both edges of
      a 32:9 screen and reads as a strip rather than a notice.
    */
    '      "d.style.cssText=\'position:fixed;left:0;right:0;top:30%;z-index:2147483600;" +',
    '        "text-align:center;pointer-events:none;font-family:Georgia,serif\';" +',
    '      "document.body.appendChild(d);}" +',
    '      "d.innerHTML=\'<div style=\\\\\'display:inline-block;padding:16px 30px;" +',
    '        "max-width:62%;background:rgba(20,16,12,.92);border:2px solid " + tone + ";border-radius:10px;" +',
    '        "color:#efe6d6;font-size:28px;line-height:1.35;letter-spacing:.03em;" +',
    '        "box-shadow:0 10px 44px rgba(0,0,0,.65)\\\\\'>\' + " + body + " + \'</div>\';" +',
    '      "d.style.display=\'\';" +',
    '      "clearTimeout(window.__tsAlertT);" +',
    '      "window.__tsAlertT=setTimeout(function(){try{d.style.display=\'none\';}catch(e){}}," + ms + ");" +',
    '      "}catch(e){}})();";',
    '    try { sp.browser.executeJavaScript(src); } catch (e) { }',
    '  } catch (e) { }',
    '})();'
  ].join('\n');
}

/*
  Put a line in front of everybody, or one person.

  The stamp is what makes it an event, the same as the swing: the same words
  twice running are the same value, and a value that has not changed draws
  nothing. tone is 'bad' for anything with a clock on it.
*/
function alertAll(text, tone, ms) {
  let online = [];
  try { online = mp.get(0, 'onlinePlayers') || []; } catch (e) { return; }
  for (let i = 0; i < online.length; i++) { alertOne(online[i], text, tone, ms); }
}

function alertOne(actorId, text, tone, ms) {
  try {
    mp.set(actorId, ALERT_PROPERTY, {
      text: String(text || ''), tone: tone === 'bad' ? 'bad' : 'good',
      ms: Number(ms) || ALERT_MS, at: Date.now()
    });
  } catch (e) { /* a person without a banner still has the chat line */ }
}

/*
  How long a swing is held before the person is let go of.

  Long enough to read as a swing, short enough that nobody feels held. The exit
  is sent regardless, because an idle with no exit leaves somebody rooted until
  they work out that moving breaks it.
*/
const SWING_MS = 2500;

/*
  HOW FAR INTO A SWING THE PICK LANDS.

  Patrick, 22 September: "you dont get the ore as it hits the vein". He is
  right, and it was not a near miss: the ore was handed over the instant the
  press arrived, before the animation had been asked for, so the pick coming
  down had nothing to do with anything.

  This is when the yield happens now. It is a number and not a measurement,
  and saying so is the point: the strike inside IdlePickaxeEnter is an
  annotation in the animation and the server cannot see annotations. The only
  thing on either machine that can is the sendAnimationEvent hook in
  thornswood-front.js, which is already watching the axe fall at a chopping
  block for exactly this reason and writes down, once each, every event it did
  not recognise. Pointing it at the pickaxe as well turns this constant into
  the real event, and that is its own entry on the list.

  Until then: early in the swing, because a pick comes down at the start of a
  2.5 second idle and not at the end of it, and well inside SWING_MS so the
  yield can never outlive the animation it belongs to.
*/
const SWING_LAND_MS = 900;

/** The animation to play, by what is being worked. See SWING_PROPERTY. */
const SWING_MINE = 'IdlePickaxeEnter';
/*
  Nothing sends this any more, and the name is kept rather than deleted. It
  was read out of IDLE 6c1df under WoodChopRoot in Skyrim.esm and it is worth
  more written down than worked out again; see chopWood for why the server
  stopped sending it.
*/
const SWING_CHOP = 'IdleWoodChopStart';
const SWING_STOP = 'IdleChairExitStart';

/** The graph's unconditional reset. See the note on SWING_PROPERTY. */
const SWING_FREE = 'IdleForceDefaultState';

/*
  Run on the person's own machine EVERY FRAME for as long as SWING_PROPERTY
  holds a value. Not on change: see the note on the property, which is where
  the reading of the client that settled that is written down. Everything
  below the state guard runs once per swing because of the guard, and for no
  other reason.

  Written as one string for the same reason lockScript is: the script cannot be
  changed once the property is made, so a change here needs a server restart,
  which a deploy is.
*/
function swingScript() {
  return [
    '(function(){',
    '  try {',
    '    var v = ctx.value;',
    /*
      THE STAMP ALONE, because an end message carries no animation.

      Patrick, 23 September, working a seam: "It's stopping the animation and
      going back in that doesn't flow smoothly". Every strike was its own
      animation with its own exit, so a spell of mining was enter, swing,
      exit, enter, swing, exit. IdlePickaxeEnter is a state you stay in, so
      now it is entered once, held for the whole spell, and left once at the
      end. The end is a message with a stamp and no anim, which is why this
      no longer asks for one. A cleared property is still at zero and still
      falls out here.
    */
    '    if (!v || !v.at) { return; }',
    /*
      ctx.state is one object, made once when the client starts and handed to
      every updateOwner script, so the key is namespaced. No state at all means
      this cannot tell a swing from the frame after it, and a swing that does
      not play is better than one that never stops: it fails shut.
    */
    '    var st = ctx.state;',
    '    if (!st) { return; }',
    '    if (st.thornswoodSwingAt === v.at) { return; }',
    '    st.thornswoodSwingAt = v.at;',
    '    var sp = ctx.sp;',
    '    var pc = sp.Game.getPlayer();',
    '    if (!pc) { return; }',
    /*
      THE END OF A SPELL: leave the state, and put the real pickaxe back
      if it was on them when the spell started.

      IdlePickaxeEnter carries its own pick, so the real one comes off at
      the start (see below). Until 27 September the end path never put it
      back: a miner who walked up with the pick on their hip, or drawn,
      finished the seam bare-handed. #611. Remembered on ctx.state under
      thornswoodSwingHadTool, set only when isEquipped was true before
      unequip, so a pick that lived only in the pack stays unequipped.
    */
    '    if (v.end) {',
    '      try { sp.Debug.sendAnimationEvent(pc, v.stop); } catch (e) { }',
    '      try { if (v.free) { sp.Debug.sendAnimationEvent(pc, v.free); } } catch (e) { }',
    '      var restore = (v.tool && st.thornswoodSwingHadTool === v.tool) ? v.tool : 0;',
    '      st.thornswoodSwingHadTool = 0;',
    '      if (restore) {',
    '        try {',
    '          var back = sp.Form.from(sp.Game.getFormEx(restore));',
    '          if (back) { pc.equipItem(back, false, true); }',
    '        } catch (e) { }',
    '      }',
    '      return;',
    '    }',
    '    if (!v.anim) { return; }',
    /*
      AND THE REAL PICKAXE GOES AWAY FIRST, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF WHAT
      THIS DID AN HOUR AGO.

      The first version equipped the tool, because the pick was missing from
      the swing and the obvious reading was that an idle mimes rather than
      drawing anything. It does not: IdlePickaxeEnter carries its own pickaxe
      the moment it is allowed to play through, which it never was while the
      animation restarted every frame. Patrick, with it working: "I have a
      pick on my hip and a pick from the animation and that looks weird also
      if I hold out my pick and do it than I have 2 picks that swing".

      So the tool is UNEQUIPPED for the spell. One pick, the animation's, and
      somebody who walked up with theirs drawn does not end up swinging two.
      Silently, and without preventing removal, so nothing here can trap
      anything on anybody.

      AND REMEMBER WHETHER IT WAS ON THEM. unequipItem of something that
      was never equipped is a no-op, but we still need to know: the end
      path only puts it back when isEquipped was true here (#611).
    */
    '    if (v.tool) {',
    '      try {',
    '        var it = sp.Form.from(sp.Game.getFormEx(v.tool));',
    '        if (it) {',
    '          var had = false;',
    '          try {',
    '            var ac = sp.Actor.from(pc);',
    '            if (ac) { had = !!ac.isEquipped(it); }',
    '          } catch (e2) { had = false; }',
    '          st.thornswoodSwingHadTool = had ? v.tool : 0;',
    '          if (had) { pc.unequipItem(it, false, true); }',
    '        }',
    '      } catch (e) { }',
    '    }',
    '    sp.Debug.sendAnimationEvent(pc, v.anim);',
    /*
      A HELD SWING DOES NOT LET GO BY ITSELF. It is one strike of a spell that
      is still going, and the exit comes as its own message when the spell
      ends. Everything that is not a spell keeps the old shape exactly: play,
      wait, exit.
    */
    '    if (v.hold) { return; }',
    // And let them go again. Utility.wait is a promise here, not a block.
    '    sp.Utility.wait((v.ms || 2500) / 1000).then(function () {',
    '      try {',
    '        var me = sp.Game.getPlayer();',
    '        if (!me) { return; }',
    '        sp.Debug.sendAnimationEvent(me, v.stop);',
    // The named exit is a transition out of one state. This one is out of any
    // state, and it is the difference between an exit and a way out.
    '        if (v.free) { sp.Debug.sendAnimationEvent(me, v.free); }',

    '      } catch (e) { }',
    '    });',
    '  } catch (e) { }',
    '})();'
  ].join('\n');
}

/*
  Ask for a swing, and take the ask back once it has been swung.

  THE STAMP IS THE WHOLE EVENT. The script above plays a swing when at moves
  and does nothing at all when it has not, so the stamp is what separates two
  strikes at the same seam, and it is the only thing that does.

  AND THE PROPERTY IS CLEARED AFTER. A swing is something that happened, not
  something a character is. Left on the character it is stored, sent to the
  owner again at the next login, and played again by a client that has never
  seen that stamp before, which is the animation nobody could get out of by
  loading a fresh game. Cleared, there is nothing to send. The clear is a
  little past the end of the swing so it cannot race the client's own exit.
*/
function swing(actorId, anim, lands, opts) {
  var o = opts || {};
  try {
    mp.set(actorId, SWING_PROPERTY,
           { anim: anim, stop: SWING_STOP, free: SWING_FREE,
             ms: SWING_MS, at: Date.now(),
             // Held for a spell of work, and the tool to put in their hands.
             hold: !!o.hold, tool: Number(o.tool) || 0 });
  } catch (e) {
    /*
      A person whose property will not take is a person without an animation,
      and NOTHING ELSE ABOUT THEIR WORK CHANGES. That sentence was here before
      the yield moved onto the strike and it has to keep being true now that it
      has: the seam has already been taken from by the time this is called, so
      falling out here would leave somebody a piece of ore short because their
      client could not be sent an idle. Everything below runs either way; only
      the animation is lost. Logged once, so a broken client does not fill the
      log.
    */
    if (!SWING_QUIET[actorId]) {
      SWING_QUIET[actorId] = true;
      log('could not ask', actorId.toString(16), 'for a swing animation:', e);
    }
  }
  SWING_IN_AIR[actorId] = true;
  // A held swing is cleared by whoever is holding it, through swingEnd. A
  // timer that cleared it would put the pick away in the middle of the spell
  // it was asked to hold.
  if (SWING_CLEAR[actorId]) { clearTimeout(SWING_CLEAR[actorId]); delete SWING_CLEAR[actorId]; }
  if (o.hold) {
    if (lands) { setTimeout(guard('swingLands', lands), SWING_LAND_MS); }
    return;
  }
  // One timer per person, not one per strike: a miner working a seam sets this
  // again every swing, and the last one is the one that clears.
  SWING_CLEAR[actorId] = setTimeout(function () {
    delete SWING_CLEAR[actorId];
    delete SWING_IN_AIR[actorId];
    /*
      A spent swing rather than null. The script above throws it away on
      either, but this property has only ever held an object and a property
      that refuses the value it is cleared with is a property that is never
      cleared, which is the whole fault. An empty anim and a zero stamp are
      what it checks for first.
    */
    try { mp.set(actorId, SWING_PROPERTY, { anim: '', at: 0 }); } catch (e) { }
  }, SWING_MS + 1000);
  if (lands) { setTimeout(guard('swingLands', lands), SWING_LAND_MS); }
}
/*
  End a held swing: leave the state and put the tool away.

  A stamp of its own, because the script edge triggers on the stamp and an
  end that reused the last one would be read as the swing it is ending.
*/
function swingEnd(actorId, tool) {
  if (SWING_CLEAR[actorId]) { clearTimeout(SWING_CLEAR[actorId]); delete SWING_CLEAR[actorId]; }
  delete SWING_IN_AIR[actorId];
  try {
    mp.set(actorId, SWING_PROPERTY,
           { anim: '', end: true, stop: SWING_STOP, free: SWING_FREE,
             tool: Number(tool) || 0, at: Date.now() });
  } catch (e) { }
  // And cleared behind it, so nothing replays at the next login.
  setTimeout(function () {
    try { mp.set(actorId, SWING_PROPERTY, { anim: '', at: 0 }); } catch (e) { }
  }, 1500);
}

const SWING_QUIET = {};
const SWING_CLEAR = {};
/* Who has a pick in the air right now. See swinging(). */
const SWING_IN_AIR = {};

/*
  Is this person already mid swing?

  Patrick, 22 September: "if you click duitig the animation you get the ore".
  A press that lands inside a swing somebody is already taking is not a second
  strike, it is the same strike clicked twice, and it used to be worth a second
  piece of ore. This is what a branch asks so it can ignore it.
*/
function swinging(actorId) { return !!SWING_IN_AIR[actorId]; }

/*
  ==================== ONE PRESS IS A SPELL OF MINING ======================

  Patrick, in the world on 23 September: "I just mines it's not doing one
  animation with multiple swings it's doing it one at a time and you have to
  keep clicking".

  He is describing vanilla, and vanilla is right: you activate a seam, your
  character works it, and you walk away when you have had enough. One press
  one swing is a clicking game.

  A SESSION IS A STAMP AND NOTHING ELSE. The person is the key, the stamp is
  what tells one spell of mining from the next, and every swing checks that
  the stamp it started under is still the one on the person. Anything that
  wants it stopped clears the entry; the swing in the air finishes and no
  other follows it. Nothing has to be cancelled, which matters because the
  swing itself is a timer on the client and cannot be taken back.

  WHAT STOPS IT, and each of these is a case somebody hit:
    - pressing again, which is how vanilla stops too
    - the seam running out, which already says so
    - the tool breaking, because the next swing has no pick behind it
    - walking away from the seam, measured rather than assumed
    - logging out, since nobody is there to swing
    - MINE_MOST swings, which is a hard stop under any circumstances

  MINE_MOST is well past the twenty a full seam holds. It is not a balance
  number, it is the thing that stops a bug from being infinite.
*/
const MINE_SESSION = {};   // actorId -> the stamp of the spell they are on
const MINE_TOOL = {};      // actorId -> the pickaxe that went into their hands
const MINE_GAP_MS = 350;   // between the end of one swing and the next
const MINE_MOST = 60;      // a hard stop, never reached by a real seam
const MINE_AWAY = 400;     // how far from the seam is walking away

/** Is this person mining right now? */
function mining(actorId) { return !!MINE_SESSION[actorId]; }

/*
  Somebody who has gone is not mining.

  ASKED RATHER THAN TOLD, because there is nothing to tell it. Every event a
  handler in this file can answer is listed over mp.onTakeItem, and a
  disconnect is not one of them, so a spell of mining cannot be ended by
  somebody leaving. It is ended by the next swing noticing they are not here.
  A list that cannot be read answers yes: the other five stops still apply and
  the hard cap is under all of it.
*/
function miningGone(actorId) {
  try {
    const on = mp.getOnlinePlayers() || [];
    return on.indexOf(actorId) < 0;
  } catch (e) { return false; }
}

/*
  Stop whatever spell of mining this person is on.

  The held animation is left here and nowhere else, because it is held: there
  is no timer under it that would end it on its own, which is the whole point
  of holding it. Everything that stops a spell comes through this.
*/
function stopMining(actorId) {
  if (!MINE_SESSION[actorId]) { return; }
  delete MINE_SESSION[actorId];
  const tool = MINE_TOOL[actorId] || 0;
  delete MINE_TOOL[actorId];
  swingEnd(actorId, tool);
}

/*
  Still at the seam?

  Both positions come off the server, which has had them for everybody since
  the first day, so this asks nothing of the client. A reference whose
  position cannot be read answers yes rather than no: refusing to keep
  swinging because a lookup failed would stop a spell of mining for a reason
  nobody could see, and the other five stops still apply.
*/
function stillAt(actorId, refrId) {
  try {
    const me = mp.get(actorId, 'pos');
    const it = mp.get(refrId, 'pos');
    if (!me || !it || me.length < 3 || it.length < 3) { return true; }
    const dx = me[0] - it[0], dy = me[1] - it[1], dz = me[2] - it[2];
    return Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy + dz * dz) <= MINE_AWAY;
  } catch (e) { return true; }
}

const DEPOSIT_PROPERTY = 'thornswoodDeposit';
/* Seams whose last write threw. See takeFromDeposit. */
const DEPOSIT_UNWRITABLE = {};

/*
  How long a worked out seam takes to come back, by what it gives.

  Patrick's two numbers, and the ores he named for each. Anything the list does
  not name falls to the LONGER one, because a seam nobody has classified being
  the cheapest tap in the province is the failure that matters.
*/
const DEPOSIT_BACK_MS = { quick: 10 * 60000, slow: 30 * 60000 };
const DEPOSIT_QUICK = ['iron', 'gold', 'corundum', 'silver'];
/*
  THE SLOW LIST IS CHECKED FIRST, AND QUICKSILVER IS WHY.

  'Quicksilver' CONTAINS 'silver'. Matching the quick list first put a
  quicksilver seam on the ten minute clock, which is one of the five Patrick
  named for thirty. Caught by the harness on the first run, which is the only
  reason it is not live: it reads perfectly and is wrong.

  So the long list wins any tie, and the default is long too. Both point the
  same way on purpose: a seam nobody has classified being the cheapest tap in
  the province is the failure that matters.
*/
const DEPOSIT_SLOW = ['moonstone', 'quicksilver', 'orichalcum', 'ebony', 'malachite'];

function depositBackMs(name) {
  const n = String(name || '').toLowerCase();
  for (let i = 0; i < DEPOSIT_SLOW.length; i++) {
    if (n.indexOf(DEPOSIT_SLOW[i]) !== -1) { return DEPOSIT_BACK_MS.slow; }
  }
  for (let j = 0; j < DEPOSIT_QUICK.length; j++) {
    if (n.indexOf(DEPOSIT_QUICK[j]) !== -1) { return DEPOSIT_BACK_MS.quick; }
  }
  return DEPOSIT_BACK_MS.slow;
}

/**
 * How many are left in this deposit right now, refilling it when its time is up.
 *
 * Reading is what refills it, the same choice the property lapse and the farm
 * claims made: there is no tick that would notice sooner and nothing in the
 * world changes until somebody asks.
 */
function depositLeft(refrId, backMs) {
  let d = null;
  try { d = mp.get(refrId, DEPOSIT_PROPERTY); } catch (e) { d = null; }
  if (!d || typeof d !== 'object') { return DEPOSIT_SIZE; }
  const left = Number(d.left);
  const at = Number(d.at) || 0;
  if (!(left >= 0)) { return DEPOSIT_SIZE; }
  if (left > 0) { return left; }
  // Empty, so the only question is whether it has had long enough.
  if (at && (Date.now() - at) >= backMs) { return DEPOSIT_SIZE; }
  return 0;
}

/** Take one out, and stamp the moment it ran dry. */
function takeFromDeposit(refrId, backMs) {
  const left = depositLeft(refrId, backMs);
  if (left <= 0) { return 0; }
  const now = left - 1;
  try {
    mp.set(refrId, DEPOSIT_PROPERTY, { left: now, at: now === 0 ? Date.now() : 0 });
    delete DEPOSIT_UNWRITABLE[refrId];
  } catch (e) {
    /*
      ONCE PER SEAM, NOT ONCE PER SWING, AND NEVER SILENTLY.

      This already logged, and the log is what nobody was reading, because a
      seam that cannot be written is also a seam that reads full: the person
      sees ore coming out and nothing looks wrong from any side. Patrick got a
      hundred out of one vein before anybody noticed.

      A line per swing would be a hundred lines and would bury the first one,
      so it is the first swing on each seam that says it, and the message says
      what it MEANS rather than only what failed. The count is kept so a seam
      that starts working again is not held against it.
    */
    if (!DEPOSIT_UNWRITABLE[refrId]) {
      DEPOSIT_UNWRITABLE[refrId] = true;
      log('THE SEAM AT', refrId.toString(16), 'CANNOT BE WRITTEN, so it will ' +
          'never run out and everybody can take from it forever:', e);
    }
  }
  return now + 1;
}

/** What to say when a seam is empty, in minutes rather than seconds. */
function depositBackIn(refrId, backMs) {
  let d = null;
  try { d = mp.get(refrId, DEPOSIT_PROPERTY); } catch (e) { d = null; }
  const at = (d && Number(d.at)) || 0;
  if (!at) { return 'It will come back.'; }
  const left = Math.max(0, backMs - (Date.now() - at));
  const mins = Math.ceil(left / 60000);
  return 'It comes back in about ' + mins + (mins === 1 ? ' minute.' : ' minutes.');
}

function mineOre(actorId, refrId, baseDesc) {
  if (!isVein(baseDesc)) return null;
  const seam = ORES_BY_BASE[baseDesc];

  // The class first, and before the cooldown, the same ordering mineSalt and
  // harvest() settled on and for the same reason: somebody who is not a miner
  // is never going to work this seam, and 'it needs 14 more seconds' tells
  // them the wrong thing about why nothing happened.
  const mine = classGate(actorId, 'mine');
  if (mine) return mine;

  // The pickaxe, after the class and before the rank and the cooldown, which is
  // the same order the quarry uses. A miner standing at an ebony seam with no
  // pickaxe is told about the pickaxe first, because that is the one they can
  // do something about today.
  const tool = toolGate(actorId, 'miner');
  if (tool) return tool;

  const step = rankStep(actorId, 'miner');
  const pair = (seam.yield && seam.yield[step]) || [0, 0];
  if (pair[0] <= 0 && pair[1] <= 0) {
    return 'This seam is ' + seam.name + '. Working it takes ' + rankWord(seam.rank) +
           ' Miner. Work the seams you can until you are one.';
  }

  /*
    THE DEPOSIT, IN PLACE OF THE OLD PER-PERSON COOLDOWN.

    MINE_COOLDOWN_MS used to refuse the person for between ten minutes and four
    hours after one strike, and it was the only thing standing between a Master
    Miner and every gem seam in the province, because nothing in the engine
    depletes one. A deposit does that job better and reads as a place rather
    than a punishment: twenty in the seam, one a swing, and it comes back on
    its own clock for everybody at once.

    The clock is Patrick's: ten minutes for iron, gold, corundum and silver,
    thirty for everything else, with anything unnamed taking the longer one.
  */
  /*
    A PRESS THAT LANDS INSIDE A SWING IS NOT A SECOND STRIKE.

    Patrick, 22 September: "if you click duitig the animation you get the ore".
    Clicking four times while the pick was still up was four pieces of ore off
    a seam that had only been struck once. Nothing is said: an early click is
    not a mistake worth a sentence, and the strike they are already taking is
    about to hand them what they came for.
  */
  /*
    AND A PRESS WHILE ALREADY MINING PUTS THE PICK DOWN.

    The same button starts and stops it, which is how the seam behaves in the
    game everybody already knows. Said out loud, because a character who
    stops swinging with nothing on screen reads as the thing having broken
    again.
  */
  if (mining(actorId)) {
    stopMining(actorId);
    return 'You put the pick down.';
  }
  if (swinging(actorId)) { return ''; }

  const backMs = depositBackMs(seam.name);
  const took = takeFromDeposit(refrId, backMs);
  if (!took) {
    return 'This seam is worked out. ' + depositBackIn(refrId, backMs);
  }

  /*
    THE PICK GOES UP HERE AND THE ORE COMES OFF WHEN IT LANDS.

    Everything above this line is a refusal, and a refusal has to be immediate:
    somebody who is not a miner, has no pickaxe, is not ranked for the seam or
    is standing at a worked out one is told so on the press, the way they
    always were. Everything below it is the yield, and the yield is what moves.

    The seam is taken from on the press and not on the strike, deliberately.
    Two miners at one vein must not both be handed the last of it because both
    of them clicked before either pick came down; the pile is the shared thing
    and the strike is the private one. The cost of that ordering is a single
    piece of ore lost out of a seam if the server stops inside the 900ms, and
    a seam refills on its own clock anyway.
  */
  const spell = Date.now();
  MINE_SESSION[actorId] = spell;
  let swungThisSpell = 0;
  /*
    ONE ANIMATION FOR THE WHOLE SPELL, WITH THEIR OWN PICKAXE IN IT.

    Patrick, 23 September: "It's stopping the animation and going back in
    that doesn't flow smoothly", and "Still a pick on my hip when I am
    swinging". Both are the same shape. A swing per strike meant enter,
    swing, exit, enter, swing, exit, which is the stutter; and an idle mimes
    rather than drawing anything, which is the pick staying on the hip.
    Neither is fixed by a better number.

    IdlePickaxeEnter is a state, so the spell enters it ONCE with the tool in
    hand, strikes on a clock while it lasts, and leaves it once at the end.
    The strikes after the first send no animation at all: the character is
    already mining.
  */
  const pick = toolIdIn(actorId, 'miner');
  MINE_TOOL[actorId] = pick;

  /*
    The next swing of the same spell, or the end of it.

    Written as a function so the first swing and every one after it are the
    same code: the first is this one called immediately, which is why the
    press still takes from the seam and still refuses everything it used to
    refuse before a pick ever goes up.
  */
  const again = function () {
    if (MINE_SESSION[actorId] !== spell) { return; }
    if (miningGone(actorId)) { stopMining(actorId); return; }
    if (swungThisSpell >= MINE_MOST) { stopMining(actorId); return; }
    if (!stillAt(actorId, refrId)) {
      stopMining(actorId);
      return;
    }
    // The tool is asked for again every swing rather than once at the top:
    // a pick that broke on the last strike is a spell that ends here.
    if (toolGate(actorId, 'miner')) { stopMining(actorId); return; }
    const more = takeFromDeposit(refrId, backMs);
    if (!more) {
      stopMining(actorId);
      const done = 'This seam is worked out. ' + depositBackIn(refrId, backMs);
      notify(actorId, done);
      say(actorId, done);
      return;
    }
    swungThisSpell++;
    strike(more);
  };

  const strike = function (took) {
  const lands = function () {
    // One a swing, not the whole pile. The rank still decides WHICH seams are
    // workable at all, which is what seam.yield's pair of zeroes says.
    const yielded = 1;
    // addItem answers false in one case only, which is idOf returning 0
    // because the plugin that owns this ore is not in the load order. The
    // quarry says 'nothing you can carry' there, which would send somebody off
    // to empty their pack over a fault in the build, so this says what is
    // actually wrong and where to take it, in the shape the farming block
    // already says it in for the same fault.
    if (!addItem(actorId, seam.ore, yielded)) {
      const broken = 'The seam gives up ' + seam.name + ' and there is nothing in ' +
                     'this load order to put in your hands. Say so in the dev channel.';
      notify(actorId, broken);
      say(actorId, broken);
      return;
    }
    const rank = creditWork(actorId, 'mine');
    const worn = wearTool(actorId, 'miner');
    /*
      NOTHING SAID FOR AN ORDINARY SWING. Patrick on the chopping block, and it
      is the same complaint: "I dont want that spam everytime". A swing a second
      with a line each is a wall of text over the thing you are trying to watch.

      The three worth interrupting somebody for are kept: a rank going up, a tool
      about to break, and the last one out of the seam, because that is the
      moment it is worth walking away from.
    */
    const last = (took === 1)
      ? 'That is the last of this seam. ' + depositBackIn(refrId, backMs) : '';
    const saying = [rank, worn, last].filter(function (x) { return !!x; }).join(' ');
    if (saying) {
      notify(actorId, saying);
      say(actorId, saying);
    }
    if (took === 1) { stopMining(actorId); return; }
    /*
      The next strike, on the animation's own rhythm.

      One swing's worth from this strike to the next, so the ore keeps coming
      off the pick at the rate the pick is moving. Nothing is sent to the
      client for it: the character is already in the animation and stays in
      it, which is what stopped the stutter.
    */
    setTimeout(guard('mineAgain', again), SWING_MS);
  };

  /*
    THE FIRST STRIKE ENTERS THE ANIMATION. EVERY ONE AFTER IT DOES NOT.

    hold keeps the client in IdlePickaxeEnter with the pickaxe in hand until
    stopMining sends the one exit, so a spell of mining is one animation with
    many strikes in it rather than many animations.
  */
  if (swungThisSpell <= 1) {
    swing(actorId, SWING_MINE, function () { lands(); }, { hold: true, tool: pick });
  } else {
    setTimeout(guard('mineLands', function () { lands(); }), SWING_LAND_MS);
  }
  };

  swungThisSpell = 1;
  strike(took);
  /*
    ONE ANIMATION, AND THIS IS WHY IT IS NOT ALLOW ANY MORE.

    Patrick, 22 September: "tehre are two". There were. ALLOW went on this
    branch to stop `return false` throwing the mining animation away, and then
    swing() was added above when that turned out not to have brought one back.
    The note at SWING_PROPERTY says why it could not have: a vein's animation
    is MineOreScript's and no Papyrus object script runs here, so for an ACTI
    vein ALLOW hands the press to an engine that does nothing with it.

    For a vein that is a FURN record it is not nothing. The table is built out
    of the ACTI AND FURN records carrying MineOreScript, and furniture does not
    need Papyrus: the press puts the person into Skyrim's own mining furniture,
    which is the second animation, the one that holds a real pickaxe, and a
    second way to be stuck standing at a seam. Two patches for one symptom,
    both left in.

    The swing above is the one that works at all 581 seams, ACTI and FURN
    alike, so it is the one that is kept, and this goes back to blocking the
    handover the way every other branch does. Empty rather than a sentence:
    there is nothing to tell somebody about an ordinary strike.
  */
  return '';
}

/**
 * Charcoal kiln: firewood in, graded charcoal out. The Charburner RANK sets
 * the grade.
 *
 * NOT a class verb, and this is the one gate that is a profession's rather
 * than a class's. classes.md gives the five classes loot, fish, gather, mine
 * and farm, and nothing else; the kiln takes firewood somebody already has and
 * turns it into charcoal, which is making rather than producing, and the
 * rulebook puts it under Woodworker: 'Charburner, firewood through to coke'.
 * So the question is canWork('charburner'), not canDo. A Woodwright answers
 * yes at novice, because 'you get the novice work of your sister half for
 * nothing', and gets the plain grade for the same reason: the grade is the
 * rank now, where it used to be the woodcutting manual's tier. 'Each rank
 * opens more of what you are able to make.'
 */
/*
  THE CHOPPING BLOCKS. THE WOODWRIGHT'S VERB, AND THE ONLY ONE THEY HAVE.

  Patrick, 21 September, correcting an earlier attempt of mine that overrode all
  three of these records into crafting benches: "The chopping block shouldn't be
  a bench just for getting the firewood and not usable by non woodworkers and
  also have the animation script". So the plugin does not touch them at all, the
  records stay Bethesda's, and the whole of this is server side.

  THE THREE RECORDS, read out of Skyrim.esm on 21 September rather than
  remembered. All three are FURN with WBDT 00ff, which is 'not a bench', which is
  why standing at one has never opened anything:

    0x07022E  WoodChoppingBlock          the common one
    0x0A91BF  WoodChoppingBlockSnow      the same block in the snow regions
    0x10F575  WoodChoppingBlockCOPY0000  a stray duplicate, and the one that is
                                         missing FurnitureWoodChoppingBlock

  AND A FOURTH, IN Dragonborn.esm, added 24 September:

    0x03828B  DLC2WoodChoppingBlockAsh   Solstheim's, three placed, all in
                                         DLC2SolstheimWorld

  The chopping blocks fix of 24 September read every placed block in the load
  order and found this base missing here, so the three on Solstheim were
  outside the Woodwright rules: a press fell through every branch of the
  activate chain to loot(), which answers nothing for a thing that is not an
  actor, and the engine sat whoever pressed it down to swing, whatever their
  trade, while chopWood never ran and no Woodwright was paid. Read out of the
  plugins the same day (C:\Thornswood\Game\Data and the profile's plugins.txt,
  last one wins, nothing overrides it): the same model as WoodChoppingBlock,
  the same three keywords including FurnitureWoodChoppingBlock, the same WBDT
  00ff, the same markers and the same ResourceFurnitureScript. It is the common
  block with ash on it.

  All four are here because a person cannot tell one chopping block from
  another, which is the same reason TOOLS.farmer accepts both shovels.

  WHY IT IS THE WOODWRIGHT'S AND NOT THE WOODWORKER'S. The kiln gates on
  'charburner' and a Woodwright gets its novice grade for nothing, because 'you
  get the novice work of your sister half for nothing'. This is that rule the
  other way round: the block is the Woodwright's and a Charburner may chop at
  novice. Until today the Woodwright had no verb and no bench in the entire load
  order, so half of the Woodworker was a title with nothing behind it.
*/
const CHOPPING_BLOCKS = ['7022e:Skyrim.esm', 'a91bf:Skyrim.esm', '10f575:Skyrim.esm',
                         '3828b:Dragonborn.esm'];

/** True when this base is one of the four chopping blocks. */
function isChoppingBlock(baseDesc) {
  for (let i = 0; i < CHOPPING_BLOCKS.length; i++) {
    if (CHOPPING_BLOCKS[i] === baseDesc) { return true; }
  }
  return false;
}

/**
 * A chopping block: firewood out, and nothing in. The Woodwright's verb.
 *
 * NOT a bench and not a crafting menu. Patrick, 21 September: it is "just for
 * getting the firewood and not usable by non woodworkers". So it is answered
 * here the way the quarry and the kiln are, and the four records in Skyrim.esm
 * and Dragonborn.esm are left exactly as Bethesda wrote them. See CHOPPING_BLOCKS.
 *
 * THE YIELD RISES WITH THE RANK, the same shape mineSalt uses, because the whole
 * of the wood chain hangs off this: firewood is what the kiln burns and what a
 * handle is cut from, so a Master Woodwright supplying a village has to be worth
 * more per swing than a novice. One to two at novice, up to one to five at
 * Master.
 *
 * THE AXE IS ASKED FOR under the profession rather than the half, which is what
 * the kiln does and for the same reason: both halves of the Woodworker are
 * woodworkers and neither of them chops wood without an axe. It also means the
 * axe wears here, which is the point of a tool that breaks: the trade that uses
 * it hardest is the one that buys the most of them.
 */
/*
  WHO IS STANDING AT WHICH BLOCK, AND WHEN THEY LAST PRESSED IT.

  Patrick, 21 September: "the chopping block is still not always doing the
  animation and not always giving the wood apon hit still".

  The server sees a PRESS. The block is a FURN record, so pressing it puts the
  person into Skyrim's own chopping furniture and the axe falls over and over
  while the server hears nothing at all, and vanilla's own block script, which
  would have handed wood over per swing, does not run here any more than
  MineOreScript does. So one press was one handover and everything after it was
  a person swinging an axe at a server that had stopped listening.

  The client can see each swing, because the animation events pass through it.
  It reports them on the one channel that exists, the chat overlay's socket,
  as /chopped. That channel is a chat line, so anybody could type the word, and
  this is what stops that being worth anything: a /chopped is only credited to
  somebody the server has just watched press a chopping block, and no faster
  than an axe can actually fall.
*/
const CHOP_AT = {};
/* A press is good for this long. Longer than anybody chops in one go, short
   enough that it is not a licence to type the word an hour later. */
const CHOP_WINDOW_MS = 120000;
/* The fastest an axe falls. Two a second is not an axe, it is a chat macro. */
const CHOP_MIN_MS = 1200;

/*
  One swing's worth, reported by the client rather than pressed.

  Every gate chopWood applies is applied again here, by calling chopWood
  itself: the half, the tool, the rank and the wear all have to be asked again
  because a session can outlast any of them. A tool that broke on the last
  swing stops the next one, which is the whole point of counting swings.
*/
function choppedSwing(actorId) {
  const at = CHOP_AT[actorId];
  if (!at) { return null; }
  const now = Date.now();
  if (now - at.at > CHOP_WINDOW_MS) { delete CHOP_AT[actorId]; return null; }
  if (now - (at.last || 0) < CHOP_MIN_MS) { return null; }
  at.last = now;
  return chopWood(actorId, at.refrId, true);
}

/*
  ============ THREE BLOCKS THAT ARE NOT OURS TO WORK, BY WHERE THEY STAND ===

  Djinn, 22 September, in "Removal from area - Woodworking chopping Blocks":

    Solitude:          -64263, 106003.5, -8377.8
    Solitude Stables:  -76193.3, 96600.6, -11435
    Dragon Bridge:     -101780.7, 80484.5, -12522.7

  BY POSITION, BECAUSE THE BASE IS SHARED. CHOPPING_BLOCKS is four BASE
  records and every block in Skyrim is one of them, so there is no way to name
  one block without naming all of them. A placed reference cannot be deleted
  from here either: that is a change to a plugin, which is a rebuild and a
  pack and a server that will not start if it goes wrong. A place is something
  this file can hold, the same way a notice board is a place, and it costs one
  distance check on a press that was going to do work anyway.

  THE RADIUS IS GENEROUS ON PURPOSE. 300 units is about two paces wider than a
  block and its stump, and the nearest block Djinn did not name is a hold away
  from any of these, so there is nothing for it to catch by accident.

  WHAT A PRESS DOES NOW: nothing, and it says so once. The activate chain
  blocks the handover, so Skyrim's own furniture does not start either and
  nobody is left swinging at a block that pays nothing.
*/
const BLOCKS_OFF = [
  { where: 'Solitude', pos: [-64263, 106003.5, -8377.8] },
  { where: 'Solitude Stables', pos: [-76193.3, 96600.6, -11435] },
  { where: 'Dragon Bridge', pos: [-101780.7, 80484.5, -12522.7] }
];
const BLOCK_OFF_REACH = 300;

/** The named site a block is standing on, or null. See BLOCKS_OFF. */
function blockIsOff(refrId) {
  let pos = null;
  try { pos = mp.get(refrId, 'pos'); } catch (e) { return null; }
  if (!pos) { return null; }
  for (let i = 0; i < BLOCKS_OFF.length; i++) {
    const b = BLOCKS_OFF[i];
    const dx = pos[0] - b.pos[0], dy = pos[1] - b.pos[1], dz = pos[2] - b.pos[2];
    if (Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy + dz * dz) <= BLOCK_OFF_REACH) { return b.where; }
  }
  return null;
}

function chopWood(actorId, refrId, fromSwing) {
  /*
    #842: Skyrim starts the chopping FURN on the client the moment E is
    pressed. mp.onActivate returning false only stops the server's
    ProcessActivateNormal; the axe loop is already running locally. A
    refused press (wrong half, no axe, off-limits block, or no firewood
    form) must kick that loop the same way a mining spell ends:
    IdleChairExitStart then IdleForceDefaultState via swingEnd.
  */
  // Before the class and the axe, because this block is not work for anybody
  // and telling a woodworker they lack a tool at one would be a lie.
  const off = blockIsOff(refrId);
  if (off) {
    swingEnd(actorId);
    return fromSwing ? '' : 'Nobody works this block. There are others in ' + off + '.';
  }

  const half = halfGate(actorId, 'woodwright', 'novice',
    'A chopping block is a woodworker\'s. Only a woodworker who took Woodwright can ' +
    'work one, and a charburner gets the novice yield. Ask one.');
  if (half) { swingEnd(actorId); return half; }

  const tool = toolGate(actorId, 'woodworker');
  if (tool) { swingEnd(actorId); return tool; }

  /*
    NO WAIT BETWEEN SWINGS, because a swing is the unit now.

    There was twenty seconds between presses, which is right for a press that
    hands over a pile and wrong for one that hands over a split. Patrick asked
    for wood each time the axe falls, so the only thing left to limit it is how
    long somebody wants to stand there swinging, which is the point.
  */

  /*
    TWO A SPLIT, EVERY SPLIT, AND NOTHING SAID ABOUT IT.

    Patrick, 21 September, after playing it: "it outs alot spam like you did
    this amnouint of tiem Idont want that spam everytime", and "it should give 2
    each time the wood is split udring the looping animation".

    THE YIELD. It was randInt(1, 2 + step), so a novice got one or two and it
    changed every time. Two, flat, per split. A rank still matters because a
    higher rank splits are not what this measures; the rank shows up in what the
    charcoal kiln gives and in the recipes the half unlocks.

    THE SPAM. Every press said "You split N firewood", plus the rank line and
    the tool-wear line, and the chopping animation loops, so a minute of
    chopping was a wall of text. Nothing is said for an ordinary split now. The
    two lines that actually matter are kept: a rank going up is news, and a tool
    about to break is a warning somebody needs before it happens.

    WHAT IS STILL NOT RIGHT, said here rather than pretended: the server sees
    one press, not one swing. Vanilla's own block script hands over its firewood
    at the end of the animation, which is the "one at the end" Patrick saw. Per
    swing needs the client to tell the server each time the axe falls, and that
    is a plugin change and its own entry.
  */
  const yielded = 2;
  if (!addItem(actorId, ITEMS.firewood, yielded)) {
    swingEnd(actorId);
    return 'The block gives up firewood and there is nothing in this load order to ' +
           'put in your hands. Say so in #bugs.';
  }
  /*
    The press is what starts a session, and the block is a FURN so Skyrim's own
    furniture plays the swing. THE SERVER ASKS FOR NO ANIMATION HERE AT ALL.

    It used to ask for one on every reported swing, and that was the wrong way
    round. The report only ever arrives DURING the furniture loop, because the
    client counts the falls of an axe the engine is already swinging, so
    IdleWoodChopStart landed in the middle of the animation it was meant to
    start and restarted it. Patrick, 21 September: "the chopping block is still
    not always doing the animation". That is what the stutter was, and with the
    frame loop at SWING_PROPERTY on top of it the restart was every frame.

    A seam is the other case and it is why swing() still exists: nothing in the
    engine animates a press on a vein, so the server has to.
  */
  if (!fromSwing) {
    CHOP_AT[actorId] = { refrId: refrId, at: Date.now(), last: Date.now() };
  }
  const rank = creditWork(actorId, 'woodwright');
  const worn = wearTool(actorId, 'woodworker');
  // Only when there is something worth interrupting them for, and the press
  // goes on being a press so Skyrim swings the axe.
  const saying = ((rank || '') + (rank && worn ? ' ' : '') + (worn || '')).trim();
  if (saying) {
    notify(actorId, saying);
    say(actorId, saying);
  }
  return ALLOW;
}

/*
  AND THE SERVER HOLDS THE THREE BLOCKS DISABLED (#495).

  thornswood-front.js has taken them out of the world on each person's own
  machine since 24 September, and had to do it again every four seconds:
  the client re-enabled every Skyrim.esm reference the server streamed that
  the server did not say was disabled, because the server said so as
  "isDisabled" and the client read "disabled". Fork 258fac47 reads the right
  name. With the server itself holding the three disabled, every client on
  that build is told so when the block streams in, and it stays gone without
  the loop. The disabled flag is on the reference's change form, so it
  survives a restart; setting it again at every start costs one read each.
  The plugin's loop stays for launchers from before that client.
*/
const BLOCKS_OFF_REFS = ['d6ae9:Skyrim.esm', 'a9ee1:Skyrim.esm', 'abf83:Skyrim.esm'];

function disableBlocksOff() {
  let took = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < BLOCKS_OFF_REFS.length; i++) {
    const id = idOf(BLOCKS_OFF_REFS[i]);
    if (!id) { log('chopping block', BLOCKS_OFF_REFS[i], 'is not in this load order, left alone'); continue; }
    try {
      if (!mp.get(id, 'isDisabled')) { mp.set(id, 'isDisabled', true); took++; }
    } catch (e) {
      log('could not hold chopping block', BLOCKS_OFF_REFS[i], 'disabled:', e);
    }
  }
  if (took) { log('the server holds', took, 'chopping block(s) out of the world'); }
  return took;
}
disableBlocksOff();

/*
  Who has been told what a kiln will cost them, and when. See burnCharcoal.
*/
const KILN_ARMED = {};
const KILN_ARM_MS = 15000;

function burnCharcoal(actorId, refrId) {
  const half = halfGate(actorId, 'charburner', 'novice',
    'A kiln is a charburner\'s. Only a woodworker who took Charburner can run one, ' +
    'and a woodwright gets the plain grade. Ask one.');
  if (half) return half;

  // The woodcutter's axe, and it is asked for under the profession rather than
  // the half: both halves of the Woodworker are woodworkers, a woodwright runs
  // a kiln at the plain grade, and neither of them does it without an axe.
  const tool = toolGate(actorId, 'woodworker');
  if (tool) return tool;

  const wait = cooldownLeft(actorId, refrId);
  if (wait > 0) return 'The kiln is still burning. ' + wait + ' seconds.';

  const cost = 3;

  /*
    A PRESS IS NOT A DECISION, AND THIS ONE WAS SPENDING FIREWOOD LIKE ONE.

    Patrick, 21 September: "it's making charcoal automatically instantly upon
    opening the kiln and not when you click to make it".

    Every other verb in this file answers a press that can only mean one thing.
    A seam is struck, a block is chopped, a plot is sown, and if you did not
    want it you would not have walked up to it. A kiln is different because it
    SPENDS something: three firewood, out of a pack you are carrying for
    something else, for a grade of charcoal you may not have wanted yet. Walking
    past one and pressing it took them.

    WHY IT IS NOT A MENU, and this is the honest part. The kiln is a bench, it
    has a keyword, and BENCH_ACCESS gates it, so the crafting menu does open.
    There is nothing in it: charcoalT1 to T4 appear exactly twice in this whole
    file, in ITEMS and in this function, and no COBJ anywhere makes them. A
    proper menu needs four recipe records in Thornswood-Content.esp, which is
    Creation Kit work and its own entry.

    SO IT ASKS. The first press says what it will cost and what it will make.
    The second press within the window does it. That is still a press rather
    than a chat word, it cannot happen by accident, and it needs no new records.
    The window is deliberately short: this is a confirmation, not a state, and
    somebody who walked off and came back should be asked again.
  */
  var armed = KILN_ARMED[actorId];
  if (!armed || armed.refrId !== refrId || (Date.now() - armed.at) > KILN_ARM_MS) {
    KILN_ARMED[actorId] = { refrId: refrId, at: Date.now() };
    const willBe = ['charcoalT1', 'charcoalT2', 'charcoalT3', 'charcoalT4']
      [rankStep(actorId, 'charburner')] || 'charcoalT1';
    return 'The kiln will take ' + cost + ' firewood and give you ' +
           willBe.replace('charcoalT', 'grade ') + ' charcoal. Press it again to burn.';
  }
  delete KILN_ARMED[actorId];

  if (!takeItem(actorId, ITEMS.firewood, cost)) {
    return 'The kiln needs ' + cost + ' firewood.';
  }
  const grade = ['charcoalT1', 'charcoalT2', 'charcoalT3', 'charcoalT4'][rankStep(actorId, 'charburner')] || 'charcoalT1';
  addItem(actorId, ITEMS[grade], 1);
  stampCooldown(actorId, refrId);
  const rank = creditWork(actorId, 'charburner');
  const worn = wearTool(actorId, 'woodworker');
  return 'The kiln yields ' + grade.replace('charcoalT', 'grade ') + ' charcoal.' +
         (rank ? ' ' + rank : '') + (worn ? ' ' + worn : '');
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// character creation
//
// SkyMP has no connect event for the gamemode, and callPapyrusFunction runs on
// the server's own VM: Debug.Notification and Message.Show both resolve their
// target actor from the Papyrus stack, which is empty when the call comes from
// here. So there is no reliable way to put words on somebody's screen from this
// file, and a menu that asks a question and waits for the answer is out of the
// question entirely.
//
// What does work, on mechanisms already proven in this build: the inventory can
// be set from the server. The books were the interface once, and then the
// launcher was, and since 20 September the choice is made in the game, on K,
// in the panel the character block owns. This block runs the hall: the face,
// the wait, the reminder, and the trip to the town when the choices are in.
//
// The state machine is driven by a timer rather than an event, because there is
// no event. It is cheap: a property read per online person every few seconds.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/**
 * On, because Skyrim Unbound cannot do this job under SkyMP.
 *
 * Unbound ships the room, the appearance menu, the trade list and the location
 * list, and all of it works in single player. It does not survive here: the
 * client calls loadGame() when the server sends CreateActor, which reloads the
 * whole session from a synthetic save, and Unbound's start quest goes with it.
 * The room and the appearance menu run before that reload and so they work. The
 * two menus run after it and so they never appear.
 *
 * Nothing below depends on a quest surviving anything. The writs and passages
 * are inventory items, and the client plugin's message boxes sit on top of them
 * rather than replacing them.
 *
 * Set "thornswoodChargen": false in server-settings.json to switch it off.
 */
function chargenWanted() {
  try {
    const s = mp.getServerSettings();
    if (s && s.thornswoodChargen === false) {
      return false;
    }
  } catch (e) {
    // no settings object. on is the right default.
  }
  return true;
}

const CHARGEN_ENABLED = chargenWanted();

const CHARGEN_PROPERTY = 'thornswoodChargen';
/*
 * Three seconds was costing far more than three seconds.
 *
 * Creation walks a state machine one step per tick, and with the appearance
 * never arriving the path out of the hall is five of them: the state is made,
 * the face is marked, a position baseline is taken, movement is noticed against
 * that baseline, and then the wait below runs out. At three seconds a step that
 * is twelve seconds of pure tick overhead on top of every real wait.
 *
 * The work in a tick is a couple of property reads for at most five people, so
 * the rate was never about cost.
 *
 * IT IS HALF A SECOND, not the one second this comment asked for until 23
 * September. It went to 500 and the sentence naming a number was left behind,
 * which matters more here than it looks: the wait below is written in
 * milliseconds and reasoned about in ticks, so a comment claiming a one second
 * tick makes every one of those sums come out at twice the truth.
 */
const CHARGEN_TICK_MS = 500;

/**
 * How long to leave a client completely alone after it turns up online.
 *
 * A joining client is mid save-load: SkyrimPlatform writes a TESMODPLATFORM
 * save, loads it, and the game rebuilds the player's facegen from the TESNPC
 * record on the way through. Anything the server does to that actor lands in
 * the middle of it. SkyMP's own SpSnippetService already treats this as
 * dangerous, wrapping ShowRaceMenu in a one second delay and switching off form
 * view updates around it.
 */
const SETTLE_MS = 10000;

/* How long to wait for an appearance before opening the race menu, and how long
 * before giving up on it entirely and moving on. Both measured from the moment
 * the creation stage opened, not from the nudge. */

const NUDGE_MS = 120000;   // two minutes; see the nudge below

/* How long somebody has to be walking about before their creation counts as
 * finished, whatever the appearance says.
 *
 * Was eight seconds. Since the appearance stopped arriving this is not a
 * fallback any more, it is the only way anybody leaves the hall, so every
 * second of it is a second spent standing in an empty room waiting for nothing.
 * Four: still far more movement than a stray camera nudge produces, and it
 * comes off the end of a wait that already has the settle in front of it.
 *
 * Down again to two. The settle in front of it is measured from when somebody
 * comes online, not from when creation ends, and creation takes far longer
 * than the settle does, so by the time this matters the settle is long spent
 * and this wait is the entire delay anybody actually feels. At a one second
 * tick, two seconds is still two separate confirmations that a person is on
 * their feet rather than nudging a camera.
 *
 * And down again to one, which at CHARGEN_TICK_MS is 500 ms and one tick. The
 * check it gates is not "did the camera twitch", it is "is this person's
 * position or facing different from the last sample", which a menu does not
 * produce at all. A second confirmation was insurance against a thing that was
 * never happening, paid for by everybody standing in an empty room.
 *
 * The sentence above it said "at a one second tick" while the tick was half a
 * second, so the two confirmations it describes were one second and not two.
 * The number here never moved; the tick under it did. */
const MOVED_ENOUGH_MS = 500;

/* How far counts as having moved, between two consecutive samples.
 *
 * The test used to be any difference at all between two pose samples, with no
 * minimum, so the first report after the baseline counted: the ground snap
 * that lands somebody on the floor of the hall, and the angle normalisation
 * that follows a teleport, both move the numbers by a hair without anybody
 * touching anything. That read as "on their feet" while the race menu was
 * still up, and the teleport could fire under it.
 *
 * 32 units is about half a pace, and well under what a person turning on the
 * spot with the mouse produces. 10 degrees is a glance. */
const MOVED_UNITS = 32;
const MOVED_DEGREES = 10;
/* How often somebody standing in the hall with the panel still to fill in is
 * reminded, in chat, that K opens it. The panel opens itself the moment
 * creation begins; this is for the person who closed it, or whose page had
 * not mounted it yet when the server first asked. */
const CHOOSE_REMIND_MS = 30000;
/*
  How long the reminder holds off once somebody has CLOSED the panel on
  purpose.

  Djinn, 21 September, in "Beack out option": "There is no way to leave the
  character creation screen except to make a character then log out. this
  needs a resolution." He is right, and the reason is this constant. Escape
  and K have always shut the panel; thirty seconds later the reminder above
  pushed it straight back, so closing it did nothing and the only real exit
  was finishing a character you did not want and quitting.

  THE REMINDER IS NOT THE PROBLEM AND IT STAYS. It exists for the person
  whose panel never arrived, who has nothing on screen and no way on: see
  openCreation's fallback sentence. That case is real and it is why this is a
  BACK OFF rather than an off switch.

  What was missing is the difference between a panel that failed to arrive
  and one somebody read and shut. The first needs pushing again; the second
  is a person saying not now, and pushing it back at them is the whole
  complaint. So a deliberate close says so, and the reminder waits ten
  minutes instead of thirty seconds. K still opens it the moment they want
  it, and nothing about their character has been decided either way.
*/
const CHOOSE_LATER_MS = 600000;
/* Five minutes, not forty five seconds. This exists so nobody is stranded when
 * the race menu never opens, and it used to be short enough to fire on somebody
 * who was simply still making their face: it would give up and start them
 * mid-menu. Being stuck is the thing worth guarding against; taking a while
 * over a nose is not. */
const GIVEUP_MS = 300000;

/*
  AND IT WAITS WHILE THE RACE MENU IS REPORTED UP, for up to half an hour.

  Djinn, 22 September, in "Timed out": "Sitting in the selector window, it
  automatically timed me out to name choice without any input commands, then
  once I named the character it then brought back up the selector and is stuck
  there since the cursor icon F6 button does not show/initiate the cursor to
  choose anything and is now stuck on this window and have to task out exit."

  His session is in the live log: profile 61415365 went to appearance at
  19:50:42, and at 19:55:43, to the second, "appearance never arrived after
  300s, going on to the choices anyway". The same line fired twice more that
  day, 22:15:14 on live and 01:11:15 on dev, each at exactly 300 seconds. Five
  minutes was measured against a race menu that never opened, and the server
  had no way to tell that from one somebody is still using, so it fired on
  people making a face. What it does then is worse than moving them on. The
  client cannot close a race menu (remoteServer.ts in the fork: "TODO:
  Implement closeMenu in SkyrimPlatform"), so the race menu stays on screen
  and the K panel is pushed on top of it. And where there is a face on file,
  closeRaceMenu tells the server the menu is shut, so the face they finish
  afterwards arrives at a server that throws it away, as closeRaceMenu below
  explains. On 22 September that close went out whatever was on file: the
  check for a face went in at 01:12 the next morning (a06abe3).

  So the plugin says when the race menu is up (/racemenu, once when it opens
  and once a minute while it stays open), and while that is fresh the give up
  holds off. Sitting still reading a slider moves nobody on.

  FACE_SEEN_MS is how fresh the last report has to be: two and a half beats,
  so one lost line does not end the hold, while a client that crashed or quit
  stops reporting and the give up runs as it always did. A client that never
  reports at all, because its plugin is older or the race menu never opened,
  is exactly the case the five minutes were written for and still gets them.

  FACE_CAP_MS is for a race menu that is up and wedged: after half an hour of
  it being reported, the give up goes on anyway, because the panel and a home
  are then the only way out of the hall the server has.
*/
const FACE_SEEN_MS = 150000;
const FACE_CAP_MS = 1800000;

const firstSeen = new Map();

/* Skyrim Unbound's own character generation room, which is where a new game
 * already puts everybody, and the marker it stands them on. Falling back to the
 * configured start point is deliberate: if Unbound is ever removed from the load
 * order the flow should still run, just in a less tidy place. */
const HALL = {
  desc: '19bad4:Skyrim Unbound.esp',
  pos: [3755.7, -495.7, -207.7],
  rot: [0, 0, 180],
};

/*
  WHERE A NEW ARRIVAL STANDS IN EACH INN, AND WHERE THE NUMBERS CAME FROM.

  Eight of the nine arrival points below were first written by hand, and all
  eight are the same sum: the inn's front door, moved 110 units along the
  door's own heading, at the door's height and with its facing. Nobody stood
  on any of them. Whether that lands in the room depends on which way the
  door's model happens to face, and for seven of the eight it did not: it
  put the point along the wall beside the door, past the door's edge, or
  110 units behind the door, outside the room.

  1.1.41 replaced five by walking to them (Whiterun, Riften, Windhelm, Morthal
  and Winterhold). Riften's hand point was the one that had been in the room,
  and the walk kept it. Three were still the sum until 24 September:

    Solitude   -178, -1504, 128    on the line of the wall the door is set
                                   in, 54 units past the door's edge, at the
                                   height of the door's middle: in the wall
    Falkreath  673.1, -620.3, 0    110 units behind the front door
    Dawnstar   1824.3, -683.7, 0   110 units behind the front door

  Those three are now the point the game itself puts anybody on who walks in
  through that front door: the teleport carried by the outside half of the
  door (its XTEL), read out of the load order, with the facing it gives,
  which is into the room. Skyrim Unbound's own start marker in each of those
  inns is within 7, 9 and 20 units of them. No number here was made up, and a
  walk can still pick a better spot than the doorway: stand there, press the
  mark key, run Mark-Spot.ps1.

  Markarth is not the sum. It stands 386 units into the room, on the floor,
  and nothing in the records says it is wrong, so it is left as it was.

  tools/test-arrivals.js holds the nine front doors and the game's own point
  for each, and fails when an arrival is not on the room side of its door or
  not on the floor the door opens onto. On a machine with the game folder it
  reads them again from the plugins.

  Interior coordinates belong to their own cell, so two towns' points being
  close means nothing. Moorside Inn and Windpeak Inn are one layout, their
  front doors two units apart, which is the only reason Morthal's and
  Dawnstar's old points looked like one spot.
*/
const CHARGEN = {"shift":[["1be1a:Skyrim.esm",1],["1be1b:Skyrim.esm",1]],"towns":{"e80030:Thornswood-Content.esp":{"key":"whiterun","town":"Whiterun","arrival":{"cell":"WhiterunBanneredMare","desc":"1605e:Skyrim.esm","pos":[-113.6,-810,69.3],"rot":[0,0,3.7]}},"e80031:Thornswood-Content.esp":{"key":"riften","town":"Riften","arrival":{"cell":"RiftenBeeandBarb","desc":"16bdf:Skyrim.esm","pos":[-150.2,-598.2,64],"rot":[0,0,36.3]}},"e80032:Thornswood-Content.esp":{"key":"solitude","town":"Solitude","arrival":{"cell":"SolitudeWinkingSkeever","desc":"16a0e:Skyrim.esm","pos":[-293.9,-1450.6,0],"rot":[0,0,0]}},"e80033:Thornswood-Content.esp":{"key":"windhelm","town":"Windhelm","arrival":{"cell":"WindhelmCandlehearthHall","desc":"16789:Skyrim.esm","pos":[554.3,-3766.2,64],"rot":[0,0,91.9]}},"e80034:Thornswood-Content.esp":{"key":"markarth","town":"Markarth","arrival":{"cell":"MarkarthSilverBloodInn","desc":"16dfe:Skyrim.esm","pos":[-5588.5,-1925.8,-16.0],"rot":[0,0,180.0]}},"e80035:Thornswood-Content.esp":{"key":"falkreath","town":"Falkreath","arrival":{"cell":"FalkreathDeadMansDrink","desc":"3a184:Skyrim.esm","pos":[670.4,-443.1,0.5],"rot":[0,0,0]}},"e80036:Thornswood-Content.esp":{"key":"morthal","town":"Morthal","arrival":{"cell":"MorthalMoorsideInn","desc":"138ce:Skyrim.esm","pos":[1970.6,-160.2,0],"rot":[0,0,270.9]}},"e80037:Thornswood-Content.esp":{"key":"dawnstar","town":"Dawnstar","arrival":{"cell":"DawnstarWindpeakInn","desc":"13a7f:Skyrim.esm","pos":[1823.2,-534.7,0.5],"rot":[0,0,0]}},"e80038:Thornswood-Content.esp":{"key":"winterhold","town":"Winterhold","arrival":{"cell":"WinterholdTheFrozenHearth","desc":"13814:Skyrim.esm","pos":[1791.6,7.3,0],"rot":[0,0,88]}}}};

/* Something to wear while choosing. The appearance menu hands you back with an
 * empty inventory, and choosing a life naked in a stone room is nobody's idea
 * of a first morning. */
const SHIFT = CHARGEN.shift;

/* Nine towns, each with the inn a new arrival wakes up in. The one table left
 * of the sixteen trades and their kits. The class, the profession and the
 * half are the character block's (progression, further down), and nothing is
 * handed out for any of them: decided by Patrick on 20 September, the starter
 * kits are gone, and everybody begins with the shift and what they earn. */
const PASSAGES = CHARGEN.towns;

/* The panel sends a town as its short key, 'whiterun', because that is
 * stable. The records are keyed by descriptor, which is not: it carries the
 * plugin name and moves whenever the content is rebuilt. */
const TOWN_BY_KEY = (function () {
  const m = {};
  for (const desc in PASSAGES) { m[PASSAGES[desc].key] = desc; }
  return m;
})();

/** The town list as the panel shows it: key and name, in the table's order. */
function townsForPanel() {
  const out = [];
  for (const desc in PASSAGES) {
    const t = PASSAGES[desc];
    if (t && t.arrival) { out.push({ key: t.key, name: t.town }); }
  }
  return out;
}

/**
 * The shift, worn. Given the moment the face is made, which is the moment the
 * race menu has emptied the pack, and well before the teleport: an inventory
 * write and a teleport composed in the same tick reach the client as a load
 * and a set of item changes racing each other, which is how a bard once
 * arrived in Windhelm with everything but a shirt.
 */
function giveShift(actorId) {
  let inv = { entries: [] };
  try { inv = mp.get(actorId, 'inventory') || { entries: [] }; } catch (e) { return; }
  const entries = (inv.entries || []).slice();
  let added = 0;
  for (const pair of SHIFT) {
    const baseId = idOf(pair[0]);
    if (!baseId) { continue; }
    let have = false;
    for (const e of entries) { if (e.baseId === baseId) { have = true; break; } }
    if (have) { continue; }
    entries.push({ baseId: baseId, count: pair[1], worn: true });
    added++;
  }
  if (added) { mp.set(actorId, 'inventory', { entries: entries }); }

  /*
    AND PUT IT ON, which nothing has ever done.

    The worn flag above is on the inventory entry, and the client dresses
    itself out of the equipment record, which is a different field the
    gamemode cannot write. So the shift arrived in the pack and stayed there,
    which is "I came in with no clothes on" as reported on 21 and 23
    September. Asked for whether anything was added or not: somebody who
    already had the shift in their pack and was not wearing it is the same
    person with the same problem.
  */
  const toWear = [];
  for (const pair of SHIFT) {
    const baseId = idOf(pair[0]);
    if (baseId) { toWear.push(baseId); }
  }
  wearThese(actorId, toWear);

  /*
    SAID OUT LOUD, because "people are not loading in with their clothes on"
    was reported on 21 September and nothing anywhere could answer it.

    This is the only thing that dresses anybody, and it ran silently, so there
    was no way to tell apart: the shift was never handed over, the shift was
    handed over and something took it off afterwards, or the shift is in the
    pack and the client is not wearing it. Three different faults, one symptom,
    no evidence.

    What goes down is what was decided and what the pack looks like after it,
    which separates the first two. A character who was skipped past creation
    never reaches this function at all, and the absence of this line for them
    is itself the answer.
  */
  let wearing = 0;
  for (const e of entries) { if (e && e.worn) { wearing++; } }
  log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), 'shift:', added, 'handed over,',
      (SHIFT.length - added), 'already there,', entries.length,
      'thing(s) in the pack,', wearing, 'worn');
}

/**
 * The face is made. Now the panel.
 *
 * A home town is chosen in the game, on K, checked by the character block,
 * and that one choice brings them back here to arriveAt. Class and
 * profession are chosen on the same panel, in the hall or later in the
 * world. Nothing is chosen anywhere else. The launcher used to send a
 * choice ahead through the voice service and this waited on it, which is what
 * left people standing in this room when the choice went to the wrong service
 * (the dev server asks the dev voice service; the launcher told the live one,
 * 20 September) or never came at all.
 */
function beginChoosing(actorId, state, why) {
  giveShift(actorId);
  state.stage = 'choosing';
  state.at = Date.now();
  state.remindAt = Date.now() + CHOOSE_REMIND_MS;
  setChargen(actorId, state);
  closeRaceMenu(actorId);
  const opened = openCreation(actorId);
  /*
    THE PRESS K LINE IS FOR THE PANEL THAT DID NOT OPEN, AND FOR NOBODY ELSE.

    Patrick, 21 September: "during creation it shouldnt put in chat to press k
    to begin blah blah because it brings the menu up automically and that is
    chat spam and is unnescery". His own clips show it twice over, because the
    reminder below says it again every thirty seconds.

    openCreation pushes the panel onto the screen and answers whether it went.
    When it went, the person is looking at the thing the sentence describes and
    being told to press a key to open what is already open is noise. When it
    did NOT go, the sentence is the only way out of the room, so it is said
    then, and it says what has actually happened rather than pretending this is
    the normal greeting.
  */
  /*
    AND ONE LINE EITHER WAY, issue #416, once (tellOnce). The pack has just
    changed under them (giveShift) and the next pick moves them to a town,
    and neither is on the screen. The welcome is beginAppearance's.
  */
  tellOnce(actorId, 'choosing', opened
    ? 'You have been given plain clothes. Choose a home town, and you will ' +
      'wake up at its inn.'
    : 'You have been given plain clothes. The starting panel did not open by ' +
      'itself. Press K to choose where you will start. Your class and ' +
      'profession can be chosen there too, or later in the world.');
  log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), '-> choosing (' + why + ')' +
      (opened ? ', panel opened' : ', panel NOT opened'));
}

/** Ask the character block to push the panel open on this person's screen. */
function openCreation(actorId) {
  try {
    const c = globalThis.__thornswoodCharacter;
    if (c && c.beginCreation) { c.beginCreation(actorId); return true; }
  } catch (e) {
    log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), 'could not open creation:', e);
  }
  return false;
}

/**
 * The home is chosen. Off to the town.
 *
 * Called by the character block when a home is set on somebody whose stage
 * is choosing; class and profession may still be empty, and are picked in
 * the world. Returns true when they were sent.
 */
function arriveAt(actorId, townKey) {
  const state = chargenOf(actorId);
  if (!state || state.stage !== 'choosing') { return false; }
  const desc = TOWN_BY_KEY[String(townKey || '').toLowerCase()];
  const entry = desc ? PASSAGES[desc] : null;
  if (!entry || !entry.arrival) {
    log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), 'no such town', townKey, '- staying in the hall');
    return false;
  }
  const arrival = entry.arrival;
  mp.set(actorId, 'spawnPoint', {
    cellOrWorldDesc: arrival.desc, pos: arrival.pos, rot: arrival.rot,
  });
  mp.set(actorId, 'locationalData', {
    cellOrWorldDesc: arrival.desc, pos: arrival.pos, rot: arrival.rot,
  });
  state.stage = 'done';
  state.destination = entry.key;
  state.at = Date.now();
  setChargen(actorId, state);
  closeRaceMenu(actorId);
  log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), 'home =', entry.key, '(' + arrival.cell + ') -> done');
  /*
    ONE LINE AT THE DOOR OF THE INN, beside the log line and never instead of it.

    The To Do List's entry on narrating creation lists what a stranger meets in
    the first minutes: "a stone room with nobody in it, a race menu, silence,
    an unexplained teleport". This is the teleport. Until now the server
    wrote "home = whiterun" to its own log and the person got a load screen
    and a room they had never seen, with no word from anybody.

    Only this one. Patrick, 21 September, on the panel line: "that is chat
    spam and is unnescery". That line described what was already on the
    screen. This one is said once, after the one pick that ends creation, and
    it says where the server has just moved them, which nothing else does.
    The other steps have one line each since issue #416, through tellOnce,
    each saying what changed and not what is already on the screen.

    say() and not notify(), whose own comment says it may well do nothing
    from here.
  */
  say(actorId, 'Welcome to ' + entry.town + '. You start at the inn.');
  return true;
}

/* What the character block, further down the file, needs from here. Through
 * the global rather than the scope, the same way it reaches the rules, so the
 * sections stay separable. */
globalThis.__thornswoodChargen = {
  towns: townsForPanel,
  hasTown: function (key) { return !!TOWN_BY_KEY[String(key || '').toLowerCase()]; },
  isChoosing: function (actorId) {
    const st = chargenOf(actorId);
    return !!(st && st.stage === 'choosing');
  },
  /*
    IN CREATION: the face and then the panel, from the hall to the home.

    Patrick, 24 September, over a tester's screenshot of the hall with the
    needs bars drawn and drifting: "needs menu shouldnt show or be actuive
    during creation". The needs block asks this, and it is the chargen stage
    and nothing else: appearance and choosing are creation, done is the world.

    Before the stage opens there is no record at all for the first SETTLE_MS
    of a session. For that window it is the same physical fact the tick
    decides on: standing in the hall means new, because nobody logs out there.
  */
  inCreation: function (actorId) {
    const st = chargenOf(actorId);
    if (st) { return st.stage === 'appearance' || st.stage === 'choosing'; }
    if (!CHARGEN_ENABLED) { return false; }
    try { return mp.get(actorId, 'worldOrCellDesc') === HALL.desc; } catch (e) { return false; }
  },
  /** The plugin saw the race menu close. Only means something while the face is awaited. */
  faceDone: function (actorId) {
    const st = chargenOf(actorId);
    if (!st || st.stage !== 'appearance') { return false; }
    st.faceDone = Date.now();
    setChargen(actorId, st);
    log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), 'the plugin says the race menu closed');
    return true;
  },
  /*
    The plugin says the race menu is on screen right now. Only means something
    while the face is awaited; see FACE_SEEN_MS for what it holds off. The
    first report is logged and the once a minute ones after it are not.
  */
  faceOpen: function (actorId) {
    const st = chargenOf(actorId);
    if (!st || st.stage !== 'appearance') { return false; }
    const now = Date.now();
    if (!st.faceOpenAt) {
      st.faceOpenAt = now;
      log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), 'the plugin says the race menu is up');
    }
    st.faceSeenAt = now;
    setChargen(actorId, st);
    return true;
  },
  /*
    The panel was closed on purpose, so hold the reminder off. See
    CHOOSE_LATER_MS and the 'later' branch of /pick.

    Only while choosing. During appearance the panel is not what is on screen
    and the race menu machinery has its own timers; answering false there
    means the person is simply told to press K, which is true and harmless.
  */
  remindLater: function (actorId) {
    const st = chargenOf(actorId);
    if (!st || st.stage !== 'choosing') { return false; }
    st.remindAt = Date.now() + CHOOSE_LATER_MS;
    setChargen(actorId, st);
    log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), 'closed the panel on purpose; not reminding for',
        Math.round(CHOOSE_LATER_MS / 60000) + ' minutes');
    return true;
  },
  arrive: arriveAt
};

/**
 * The race menu is finished with, whatever route got us here.
 *
 * Spawn opens it, and only the appearance arriving closes it. Somebody who
 * left the hall on the movement path or on the give up path still has it set
 * open, CreateActor sends that flag on every later login, and the client dutifully
 * reopens the race menu on a character who has had a face for a week.
 */
/*
  Shut the race menu, EXCEPT ON SOMEBODY THE SERVER HAS NO FACE FOR.

  firstreconboone, 22 September: "Character's already made, however upon
  logging into game the options popped up." Every login, on a character that
  has been played for days.

  IT IS A LOOP AND THIS FUNCTION IS ONE HALF OF IT. Read out of the fork rather
  than guessed at, MpActor.cpp:

      // Actor without appearance would not be visible so we force player to
      // choose appearance
      if (changeForm.appearanceDump.empty()) {
        changeForm.isRaceMenuOpen = true;
      }

  That runs in ApplyChangeForm, which is every login. And the only place an
  appearance is ever stored is ActionListener.cpp:

      const bool isAllowed = actor->IsRaceMenuOpen();
      if (isAllowed) { actor->SetRaceMenuOpen(false); actor->SetAppearance(...); }

  So an appearance that arrives while the server thinks the menu is shut is
  THROWN AWAY. Put the two together: the engine opens the menu because there is
  no face on file, this file closes it a tick later because the character is
  obviously not new, the person makes a face anyway, the upload is refused, the
  dump stays empty, and tomorrow the engine opens it again. For ever.

  The returning branch in the tick has been logging the evidence the whole
  time: "already has a character carrying 4 things, not in the hall, NO FACE,
  so creation is skipped for them".

  So: no face on file, no closing. The menu the engine opened stays open, the
  person makes a face once, it is accepted because the flag was still set, and
  it never opens again. Leaving it open costs somebody one race menu they can
  click straight through; closing it costs them one every login for ever.
*/
function closeRaceMenu(actorId) {
  var has = false;
  try {
    var a = mp.get(actorId, 'appearance');
    has = !!(a && typeof a === 'object');
  } catch (e) {
    // A read that throws is treated as having a face, because the failure this
    // guard exists for is closing the menu on somebody who needs it and the
    // opposite failure is one extra menu once.
    has = true;
  }
  if (!has) {
    log('chargen', actorId.toString(16),
        'has no appearance on file, so the race menu is left open: closing it ' +
        'is what makes the next one be thrown away');
    return;
  }
  try {
    mp.setRaceMenuOpen(actorId, false);
  } catch (e) {
    log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), 'could not close the race menu:', e);
  }
}

/**
 * A short signature of a face, enough to tell one from another.
 *
 * "Has this character got an appearance yet" turned out to be the wrong
 * question. A client carries the last face it made and uploads it the moment it
 * connects, so somebody making their second character arrived already wearing
 * their first one's face: the server saw a finished character, skipped creation
 * and left them standing there. What matters is whether the face CHANGED since
 * the race menu was opened, which needs something to compare against.
 */
function faceMark(a) {
  if (!a || typeof a !== 'object') return '';
  try {
    return [a.raceId, a.isFemale, a.weight, a.hairColor, a.skinColor,
            a.headTextureSetId, a.name,
            (a.headpartIds || []).join('.'),
            (a.tints || []).length].join('|');
  } catch (e) {
    return '';
  }
}

function chargenOf(actorId) {
  let s = null;
  try {
    s = mp.get(actorId, CHARGEN_PROPERTY);
  } catch (e) {
    s = null;
  }
  if (!s || typeof s !== 'object') return null;
  return s;
}

function setChargen(actorId, state) {
  mp.set(actorId, CHARGEN_PROPERTY, state);
}

/*
  ONE LINE PER STEP OF CREATION, ONCE PER CHARACTER, issue #416.

  Patrick, 28 September, on these lines: "Yes I want it, the problem was it
  kept spamming the same message every like 5 seconds". The old creation line
  was said from the choosing reminder, which runs on a timer and runs again
  straight away every time the tick sees somebody afresh (a reconnect, a drop
  out of onlinePlayers, a hot reload emptying firstSeen). A line that rides a
  timer or a first sighting repeats; a line that rides a stage change should
  not, but only while the stage write sticks, and chargenTick re-reads the
  record every 500 ms.

  So the step is written on the record (told) BEFORE the line goes, and the
  line goes only when the record read back says so. A step already told is
  never told again, whatever calls this; a write that did not stick says
  nothing rather than saying it on every tick. A new character is a new actor
  with no record, so they are told again.
*/
function tellOnce(actorId, step, text) {
  const st = chargenOf(actorId);
  if (!st || (st.told && st.told[step])) { return false; }
  st.told = Object.assign({}, st.told);
  st.told[step] = Date.now();
  setChargen(actorId, st);
  const back = chargenOf(actorId);
  if (!back || !back.told || !back.told[step]) { return false; }
  say(actorId, text);
  return true;
}

/** Add or remove a set of [descriptor, count] pairs in one inventory write. */
function giveItems(actorId, pairs, sign) {
  const inv = mp.get(actorId, 'inventory') || { entries: [] };
  const entries = (inv.entries || []).slice();
  for (const pair of pairs) {
    const baseId = idOf(pair[0]);
    if (!baseId) {
      log('chargen: no form for', pair[0], '- skipped');
      continue;
    }
    const want = pair[1] * sign;
    let at = -1;
    for (let i = 0; i < entries.length; i++) {
      if (entries[i].baseId === baseId) { at = i; break; }
    }
    if (at === -1) {
      if (want > 0) entries.push({ baseId: baseId, count: want });
      continue;
    }
    entries[at].count += want;
    if (entries[at].count <= 0) entries.splice(at, 1);
  }
  mp.set(actorId, 'inventory', { entries: entries });
}

/** Put somebody in the creation hall. Once, at the moment the stage opens. */
function sendToHall(actorId) {
  const cell = idOf(HALL.desc);
  if (!cell) {
    log('chargen: the creation hall is not in this load order, staying put');
    return false;
  }
  mp.set(actorId, 'locationalData', {
    cellOrWorldDesc: HALL.desc,
    pos: HALL.pos,
    rot: HALL.rot,
  });
  return true;
}

/**
 * Open the creation stage. Deliberately does NOT touch the race menu.
 *
 * The Spawn system already calls setRaceMenuOpen, in both of its branches: on
 * the create path for a brand new character, and on the load path for one whose
 * appearance has been cleared. Calling it again from here was a second
 * ShowRaceMenu landing across the client's save load, on top of a teleport, and
 * it is the only new thing in the neighbourhood of the crash we saw. Watching
 * for the appearance to arrive is all this needs to do.
 */
function beginAppearance(actorId) {
  // The face they turned up with, so the one they make can be told from it.
  let was = '';
  try { was = faceMark(mp.get(actorId, 'appearance')); } catch (e) { was = ''; }

  // seenAt is the first sight recorded by the tick, so the reconnect check
  // does not read a brand new arrival as somebody coming back (it did, on
  // 20 September, half a second after every arrival: a wrong line and the
  // grace paid twice).
  const state = { stage: 'appearance', at: Date.now(), faceWas: was, seenAt: firstSeen.get(actorId) || 0 };

  // Moved first, asked second, and the asking happens on a later tick.
  //
  // Both used to happen here, in that order, and the order was wrong: the menu
  // opened wherever the client had finished loading, then the teleport landed
  // underneath it and dragged the whole scene through a load screen while
  // somebody was already picking a nose. Which is exactly what it looked like
  // from the chair: creation in a tavern, then the hall, then a wait, then
  // another teleport.
  //
  // startPoints already puts a new character in the hall, so the common case
  // moves nobody and the menu opens on the next pass a second or two later.
  let where = null;
  try {
    where = mp.get(actorId, 'worldOrCellDesc');
  } catch (e) {
    where = null;
  }
  if (where && where !== HALL.desc) {
    if (sendToHall(actorId)) {
      state.moved = true;
      // Long enough for the load screen the teleport just started. Asking for
      // the race menu during one is asking for nothing: showRaceMenu on a game
      // that is mid load does nothing at all and the request is gone.
      state.askAt = Date.now() + 4000;
    }
  }

  setChargen(actorId, state);
  log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), '-> appearance',
      state.moved ? '(moving to the hall, menu to follow)' : '(already in the hall)');
  // Issue #416, once (tellOnce): the stone room, and the teleport into it. The
  // race menu is not described: it is on their screen.
  tellOnce(actorId, 'hall', state.moved
    ? 'Welcome to Thornswood. You are going to the creation hall to make your character.'
    : 'Welcome to Thornswood. This is the creation hall, where you make your character.');
}

/**
 * One pass over everybody online.
 *
 * The timer owns two transitions: appearance -> choosing, because the race
 * menu closing is not an event, the appearance simply turns up on the change
 * form and this notices; and the reminder while somebody is choosing. The
 * exit from choosing is the character block's, when the last pick lands.
 */
function chargenTick() {
  if (!CHARGEN_ENABLED) {
    return;
  }
  let online;
  try {
    online = mp.get(0, 'onlinePlayers');
  } catch (e) {
    return;
  }
  if (!Array.isArray(online)) return;

  const now = Date.now();

  // Anyone who has gone offline should not keep a slot in the settle table.
  const here = new Set(online);
  for (const id of Array.from(firstSeen.keys())) {
    if (!here.has(id)) {
      firstSeen.delete(id);
    }
  }

  for (const actorId of online) {
    if (!firstSeen.has(actorId)) {
      firstSeen.set(actorId, now);

      /*
        Somebody who arrives inside a restart window is probably standing in an
        empty world, and until now nothing said so.

        A kick with no process restart behind it leaves the actor in a state
        where the reconnecting client is never sent the world. That is written
        out in full down in clearTheServer(), from 17 September: one character,
        13 reconnects, every one of them dead with no hits and no deaths and
        all the NPCs gone, and the same character fine after the 01:00 process
        restart. It was reported again on 20 September, and that session's log
        has the server says it is restarting, staying put immediately after
        ready, so the person had joined during the countdown. The site reported
        the server up with nobody online while he was standing in it.

        Joining during the window is a second way into the same state and the
        comment down there does not cover it. The server cannot refuse the
        connection from here, because there is no login hook to refuse it in,
        and it must not kick: a kick is the thing that causes this. So it says
        so, which is the difference between a broken evening and a known one.

        The same test the online writer uses, deliberately, so the two cannot
        disagree about whether a restart is happening. at covers the countdown
        and the tick after it, went covers the rest.
      */
      try {
        const r = globalThis.__thornswoodRestart;
        const inWindow = !!(r && ((r.at && now - r.at < 180000) ||
                                  (r.went && now - r.went < 180000)));
        if (inWindow) {
          log('login', actorId.toString(16),
              'arrived inside a restart window: if the world is empty this is why');
          say(actorId,
              'The server was restarting as you arrived. If nobody is about and ' +
              'the world looks empty, quit to the launcher and come back in a ' +
              'minute. It is not your game.');
        }
      } catch (e) { }

      /*
        Somebody who arrives already dead still gets a temple.

        spawnPoint is only ever written in onDeath, at the moment the body
        falls, so a death that happened while the client was disconnected, or
        during a kick window, leaves the character dead with whatever spawn
        point they had before. The SkyMP server does respawn a dead actor when
        it re-attaches, so they come back, and they come back at the default
        point rather than at the nearest consecrated ground.

        Written here, once, on the tick that first sees them. Nothing is moved
        and nothing is resurrected: this only decides where the respawn the
        server is already going to do puts them.
      */
      let dead = false;
      try { dead = !!mp.get(actorId, 'isDead'); } catch (e) { dead = false; }
      if (dead) {
        try {
          const where = {
            desc: mp.get(actorId, 'worldOrCellDesc'),
            pos: mp.get(actorId, 'pos'),
          };
          const back = resolveArrival(where);
          if (back) {
            mp.set(actorId, 'spawnPoint', {
              cellOrWorldDesc: back.desc, pos: back.pos, rot: back.rot,
            });
            log('login', actorId.toString(16), 'came back dead in', where.desc,
                '-> ' + back.cell + ' (' + back.slot + ')');
          }
        } catch (e) {
          log('login', actorId.toString(16), 'could not set a spawn point:', e);
        }
      }

      /*
        Every spell they have learned, back onto the character.

        A blessing survives logging out because Actor.AddSpell records it on
        the server and the list is re-sent on every spawn. A spell learned
        through the panel is the same call and survives the same way, so this
        is not what keeps it: this is what puts back the ones that were
        learned on a build with no form id for them, and the ones lost with a
        change form. AddSpell on a spell somebody already has does nothing, so
        the cost is a call per spell per session.

        This is the one per-session hook there is. It rides on the chargen
        tick, so it goes with it if "thornswoodChargen": false is ever set in
        server-settings.json; that is written down rather than worked around,
        because a second timer over the same list is worse.
      */
      try {
        const c = globalThis.__thornswoodCharacter;
        if (c && c.grantKnown) { c.grantKnown(actorId); }
      } catch (e) {
        log('login', actorId.toString(16), 'could not put the known spells back:', e);
      }

      // Their own profession, said back to them as they arrive, which proves
      // the route from the server to the person's own screen (issue #509).
      try {
        const c = globalThis.__thornswoodCharacter;
        const P = globalThis.__thornswoodProgression;
        const s = c && c.stateOf ? c.stateOf(actorId) : null;
        const prof = s && s.prof && P && P.has(P.TABLE.professions, s.prof)
          ? P.TABLE.professions[s.prof] : null;
        if (prof) {
          notify(actorId, 'Your profession: ' + prof.name +
                 (s.half && c.nameOf ? ', ' + c.nameOf(s.half) : '') + '.');
        }
      } catch (e) {
        log('login', actorId.toString(16), 'could not say the profession:', e);
      }

      /*
        AND PUT THEIR CLOTHES BACK ON. See wearWhatIsWorn: the pack survives
        a session and the equipment record does not always, which is how
        somebody comes back from an evening fully packed and undressed.
        Said in the log with a count, because "nobody is wearing anything"
        and "the server thinks nobody is wearing anything" are different
        faults and this is the line that tells them apart.

        This is the ask at the door. The watch that notices somebody already
        standing there undressed is dressIfBare, below, on the same tick.
      */
      try {
        const put = wearWhatIsWorn(actorId);
        log('login', actorId.toString(16), 'put', put, 'worn thing(s) back on');
      } catch (e) {
        log('login', actorId.toString(16), 'could not put the clothes back on:', e);
      }
    }
    /*
      NOBODY STANDS ABOUT UNDRESSED WAITING FOR A DOOR.

      The block above puts somebody's clothes back on as they arrive. This
      one is for anybody already in the world with nothing on, who would
      otherwise stay that way until they quit the game. Nothing is asked of
      the person and nothing appears on their screen: the server tells their
      client to equip what their own pack says they are wearing, and it
      happens.

      dressIfBare does nothing at all unless somebody is wearing NOTHING,
      and gives up after three goes. See the note over it.
    */
    try {
      const said = dressIfBare(actorId);
      if (said) { log('dress', actorId.toString(16), said); }
    } catch (e) { }

    if (now - firstSeen.get(actorId) < SETTLE_MS) {
      continue;
    }

    let state, appearance;
    try {
      state = chargenOf(actorId);
      appearance = mp.get(actorId, 'appearance');
    } catch (e) {
      continue;
    }

    if (!state) {
      let pidNew = 0;
      try { pidNew = Number(mp.get(actorId, 'profileId')) || 0; } catch (e) { pidNew = 0; }

      /*
        Somebody who already exists, decided by what they own.

        The check below this used to come first, on the stated grounds that a
        choice waiting from the launcher is proof the character was made
        seconds ago. It is not. The launcher posts a choice whenever it has a
        trade and a town recorded for a slot, and it has those for every
        character it has ever made, so a character who has been played for
        hours arrives here looking brand new. What follows is creation run a
        second time on somebody who already finished it: another full starter
        kit tipped into a pack that already had one, every piece of it equipped
        over what they were already wearing, and a trip back to the town they
        started in.

        A pack with things in it is proof in the other direction, because
        nobody arrives owning something unless they have played.

        EXCEPT GOLD, AND THAT SENTENCE USED TO READ "it cannot be wrong,
        because nothing hands anybody anything before this point". It stopped
        being true on 21 September, the same evening the starting purse was
        added, and it took creation down for everybody.

        The purse loop runs over everybody online a few lines above this one.
        A brand new character arrived, was handed forty gold, and on THE SAME
        TICK this read their pack, found one entry in it, and concluded they
        had played before. Creation was skipped, they were stamped done, and
        closeRaceMenu shut the menu Spawn had correctly opened for them. Three
        sessions in a row on the live server:

          15:19:54  chargen ff000058 profile 744947683 already has a character
                    carrying 1 thing, in the hall, no face, so creation is
                    skipped for them

        In the hall, no face, and told they had done this before. Djinn
        described it from the other side: the menu never opens after naming and
        you are stuck in the room.

        So gold does not count. The question this is asking is whether somebody
        has PLAYED, and gold the server handed out unprompted is the one thing
        in a pack that is no evidence of that. The purse also moved below the
        chargen loop, so it cannot reach the same tick's decision at all; this
        is the half that does not depend on the order being right.
      */
      const goldId = (globalThis.__thornswoodSpoils
                      && globalThis.__thornswoodSpoils.GOLD) || 0x0000000F;
      let owns = 0;
      try {
        const inv = mp.get(actorId, 'inventory');
        const entries = (inv && inv.entries) || [];
        for (let i = 0; i < entries.length; i++) {
          if (entries[i] && entries[i].baseId !== goldId) { owns++; }
        }
      } catch (e) { owns = 0; }

      /*
        New or returning is decided by two physical facts, in this order, and
        by nothing inferred.

        FIRST, THINGS IN THE PACK MEAN RETURNING, wherever they are standing.
        Nothing hands anybody anything before this point, so nobody arrives
        owning something unless they have played. This is the proof that
        cannot be wrong and it goes first. 7ffe41a demoted it and that was a
        mistake, below.

        SECOND, WITH AN EMPTY PACK, STANDING IN UNBOUND'S HALL MEANS NEW. The
        hall is where the server's start point puts a brand new actor and
        nowhere else; nobody logs out there and nobody wakes up there twice.
        Anywhere else with an empty pack is somebody returning, most often a
        legacy character from before creation existed.

        WHY NOT THE APPEARANCE. 7ffe41a made "has a face" mean "returning", to
        stop played characters being re-created in whatever inn they had last
        chosen. That fixed the inn and broke the hall: on 20 September at
        11:52:26 profile 1043928337, brand new, was logged as already having a
        character and stamped done in the hall with no teleport and no way
        out. SkyMP opens the race menu on spawn, before this tick ever sees
        the actor, and confirming a face takes less than the ten second settle,
        so a new character has an appearance by the time anyone asks. A face
        is not evidence of anything either. Neither is a launcher choice: the
        launcher posts one for every slot it has ever made and the voice
        service keeps them a week, sixty five were waiting that morning.

        A location read that throws counts as not in the hall. Re-creating a
        person is the worse of the two mistakes, so the doubt goes that way.
      */
      let inHall = false;
      try {
        inHall = mp.get(actorId, 'worldOrCellDesc') === HALL.desc;
      } catch (e) { inHall = false; }

      const returning = owns > 0 || !inHall;
      if (returning) {
        log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), 'profile', pidNew,
            'already has a character' +
            (owns > 0 ? ' carrying ' + owns + (owns === 1 ? ' thing' : ' things')
                      : ' and is carrying nothing') +
            (inHall ? ', in the hall' : ', not in the hall') +
            (appearance ? ', face on' : ', no face') +
            ', so creation is skipped for them');
        setChargen(actorId, { stage: 'done', at: now, legacy: true });
        closeRaceMenu(actorId);
        // Issue #416, once (tellOnce): why the race menu shut, if it was up.
        tellOnce(actorId, 'back', 'Welcome back to Thornswood. This character is already made.');
        continue;
      }

      // Genuinely new: an empty pack, standing in the hall.
      log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), 'profile', pidNew,
          'is new, in the hall with nothing' +
          (appearance ? ', face already on' : ''));
      beginAppearance(actorId);
      continue;
    }

    /*
      Bard is RP-only now (see RP_ONLY), not a Class. The old instrument
      ability that was handed out for that class key is gone: nobody gets
      PlayInstrumentSpell for having been a bard. Playing for rest is /bard;
      instruments themselves are woodwright craft and the emote wheel.
    */

    // 'arriving' was the kit top-up after the teleport. There are no kits, so
    // anybody still marked arriving from before 20 September is simply done.
    if (state.stage === 'arriving') {
      state.stage = 'done';
      setChargen(actorId, state);
      closeRaceMenu(actorId);
      log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), 'was marked arriving from before the kits went; closed');
      continue;
    }

    /*
      Standing in the hall with the panel to fill in.

      The exit is not here: the character block sends them the moment the
      home is picked (arriveAt). This is the reminder, and the reopen after
      a reconnect, because the page that had the panel is gone with the old
      session and the property does not fire again on its own.
    */
    if (state.stage === 'choosing') {
      const cameOnC = firstSeen.get(actorId) || 0;
      if (state.seenAt !== cameOnC) {
        state.seenAt = cameOnC;
        state.remindAt = now;
        setChargen(actorId, state);
      }
      if (now < (state.remindAt || 0)) { continue; }
      state.remindAt = now + CHOOSE_REMIND_MS;
      setChargen(actorId, state);
      /*
        THE REMINDER STILL PUSHES THE PANEL, SILENTLY. See beginChoosing.

        This is the rescue for a panel that never arrived, and pushing it again
        costs the person nothing. Saying so every thirty seconds cost them a
        chat window, which is what Patrick's clips show. The sentence is kept
        for the case it was written for: the push failed, so there is nothing
        on their screen and a key press is the only way on.
      */
      //
      // And said once (tellOnce, issue #416): this is the timer that repeated
      // the old line, and beginChoosing has already said it on the same step,
      // so a reminder, a reconnect or a hot reload after that says nothing.
      if (!openCreation(actorId)) {
        tellOnce(actorId, 'choosing', 'The starting panel did not open. Press K ' +
                     'to choose where you will start. Your class and profession ' +
                     'can wait until you are there.');
      }
      continue;
    }

    if (state.stage === 'appearance') {
      /*
        A reconnect starts the wait again from nothing.

        None of the fields below are cleared when somebody drops out of the
        hall and comes back, so a character who walked about for a few seconds
        and then quit returns with liveSince already set, where holding a
        position from the last session, and askedAt already past. The very
        first settled tick then reads that as somebody who has been on their
        feet for as long as they were away, and teleports them to the inn
        while their client is still loading the hall.

        firstSeen is set the moment somebody comes online, so comparing it
        against what the state remembers is what tells a reconnect from the
        session that made it.
      */
      const cameOn = firstSeen.get(actorId) || 0;
      if (state.seenAt !== cameOn) {
        state.seenAt = cameOn;
        delete state.liveSince;
        delete state.where;
        delete state.askedAt;
        delete state.nudged;
        // The last session's race menu went with it. This one reports its own.
        delete state.faceOpenAt;
        delete state.faceSeenAt;
        delete state.faceHeld;
        // Long enough for the arrival to settle before a baseline is taken,
        // the same grace a fresh arrival gets.
        state.askAt = now + 4000;
        setChargen(actorId, state);
        log('chargen', actorId.toString(16),
            'came back mid creation, so the wait starts again');
        continue;
      }

      // The baseline, once whatever load screen we started has had time to
      // finish. No menu is opened here, deliberately. See below.
      if (!state.askedAt) {
        if (now < (state.askAt || 0)) { continue; }
        state.askedAt = now;
        // The face they are standing in right now, which after a teleport is
        // the one the client re-sent on arrival. Taken here rather than before
        // the move so that arrival is not mistaken for a finished face.
        state.faceWas = faceMark(appearance);
        setChargen(actorId, state);
        continue;
      }

      // Whether they are on their feet or in a menu, which is the one thing
      // that decides whether opening the race menu would be a rescue or a
      // wrecking ball. The server cannot ask the client what is on screen, but
      // it can see that somebody is turning on the spot: the angle moves every
      // frame while a person has the mouse, and not at all while a menu has it.
      let live = false;
      try {
        const pos = mp.get(actorId, 'pos') || [0, 0, 0];
        const ang = mp.get(actorId, 'angle') || [0, 0, 0];
        const here = [pos[0], pos[1], pos[2], ang[2]].join(',');
        if (state.where && state.where !== here) {
          // Far enough to be a person, not a settle. See MOVED_UNITS.
          const was = String(state.where).split(',');
          const dx = pos[0] - Number(was[0]);
          const dy = pos[1] - Number(was[1]);
          const dz = pos[2] - Number(was[2]);
          let turned = Math.abs(ang[2] - Number(was[3]));
          if (turned > 180) { turned = 360 - turned; }
          const moved = Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy + dz * dz);
          if (moved >= MOVED_UNITS || turned >= MOVED_DEGREES) { live = true; }
        }
        if (state.where !== here) {
          state.where = here;
          // Saved, not just assigned.
          //
          // This is the whole bug. `state` is read back off the actor at the
          // top of every tick, so a field written to the local copy and not
          // passed to setChargen is gone three seconds later. state.where was
          // therefore undefined on every single tick, the comparison above it
          // never had a baseline to compare against, live was never true, and
          // liveSince was never set.
          //
          // Everything after this point is gated on liveSince: the movement
          // path, the two minute nudge that reopens the race menu, and the
          // five minute give up that starts you anyway. All three were dead.
          // Nobody was ever going to leave that room, on any timescale, and
          // the log could not say so because the code that would have logged
          // it was behind the same gate.
          setChargen(actorId, state);
        }
      } catch (e) { }
      if (live && !state.liveSince) {
        state.liveSince = now;
        setChargen(actorId, state);
        log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), 'is on their feet');
      }

      // Changed, not merely present. See faceMark above.
      //
      // And a change here is exact, not a guess. SendInputsService.sendAppearance
      // is the only thing in the client that ever sends one:
      //
      //     var shown = this.sp.Ui.isMenuOpen('RaceSex Menu');
      //     if (shown != this.isRaceSexMenuShown) {
      //       this.isRaceSexMenuShown = shown;
      //       if (!shown) { ...send... }
      //     }
      //
      // One transition, open to closed, nothing else anywhere. So once the menu
      // has been asked for, the next appearance to arrive IS the finished face,
      // whether it took eight seconds or eight minutes.
      //
      // There was a twelve second window here that ignored early changes,
      // added because creation once flashed up and teleported instantly. That
      // was the wrong cure for the right symptom: the real cause was this
      // function teleporting somebody while their race menu was open, which
      // closed the menu, which made the client send the unfinished face. The
      // teleport happens before the menu is asked for now, so there is nothing
      // left to ignore, and ignoring things cost somebody who finished quickly
      // their exit from the hall: the window kept moving the mark forward, so
      // the face they had just made became the face they started with.
      const faceNow = faceMark(appearance);
      const faceIsNew = !!appearance && faceNow !== (state.faceWas || '');

      if (faceIsNew) {
        beginChoosing(actorId, state, 'the face arrived');
        continue;
      }

      // The plugin says the race menu closed (/face done, over the chat
      // socket). That is the fact the mark above is only a proxy for: a face
      // confirmed unchanged, or a name kept, changes no mark, and until 20
      // September the only way on from there was to walk (below). The menu
      // closing is enough on its own.
      if (state.faceDone) {
        beginChoosing(actorId, state, 'the race menu closed');
        continue;
      }

      // Walking around the hall is the end of character creation.
      //
      // Everything above waits on one signal: the appearance property changing
      // on the change form, which is what arrives when the race menu closes.
      // When that signal does not come, the whole flow stops, and the log says
      // exactly that and nothing else: "-> appearance (already in the hall)"
      // and then silence while somebody stands in a stone room wondering what
      // they did wrong.
      //
      // The rescue below existed for that, and it is far too patient: two
      // minutes before it reopens the race menu, five before it gives up and
      // starts them anyway. Nobody waits two minutes in an empty room. They
      // alt-F4, which is what happened three times tonight.
      //
      // But the evidence was there the whole time. The race menu is paused, so
      // a character in one does not move; a character who IS moving is a
      // character whose menu has closed. That is not a guess about the
      // appearance, it is a stronger fact than the appearance, and this file
      // was already measuring it a dozen lines up in order to decide whether
      // nudging was safe.
      //
      // So: on your feet, and we get on with it. The appearance can arrive
      // whenever it likes.
      const onFeetFor = state.liveSince ? (now - state.liveSince) : 0;

      if (onFeetFor > MOVED_ENOUGH_MS) {
        // Said in full because the appearance not arriving is still a bug
        // and this is the only place it can be seen from.
        log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), 'walking after',
            Math.round(onFeetFor / 1000) + 's and the face never arrived.',
            'appearance is', appearance ? 'present' : 'MISSING,',
            'mark now', JSON.stringify(faceNow) + ',',
            'mark at the start', JSON.stringify(state.faceWas || '') + '.',
            'Going on the movement instead.');
        beginChoosing(actorId, state, 'on their feet');
        continue;
      }

      // Nobody is ever left standing in the hall with nothing to do.
      //
      // This step waits for an appearance to turn up on the change form,
      // because the race menu closing is not an event. That is fine right up
      // until the race menu never opens, and then it waits forever: no menu,
      // no writs, no way forward, and a room with a person in it. Spawn is
      // supposed to open it on both of its branches, and when it does not the
      // reason is somewhere in a client mid save-load, which is not somewhere
      // this can reach.
      //
      // So: ask once, then stop waiting. A character who never saw the race
      // menu can open it later. One who cannot leave the room cannot do
      // anything at all.
      //
      // But only once it is clear that nobody is standing in a menu already.
      //
      // Spawn opens the race menu itself for every character it creates:
      //
      //     actorId = ctx.svr.createActor(...);
      //     ctx.svr.setRaceMenuOpen(actorId, true);
      //
      // so asking for it here as a matter of course was asking for a second
      // one. Which is what it did: a face was made, done was pressed, and the
      // menu opened again. Then the game went down with an access violation
      // trying to execute freed memory inside
      // NativeFunction1<TESNPC, BGSHeadPart*, unsigned long>, which is a head
      // part call on a character whose last race menu had already been torn
      // down. The second menu and the crash were the same thing.
      //
      // So this is a rescue and nothing else, and it only fires for somebody
      // who is demonstrably on their feet with no face, which is exactly the
      // case it was written for.
      const waited = now - (state.liveSince || state.askedAt || state.at || now);

      /*
        THE GUARD BELOW USED TO COVER BOTH RESCUES AND IT MADE NEITHER OF THEM
        REACHABLE BY THE PEOPLE THEY WERE WRITTEN FOR.

        It read `if (!state.liveSince) { continue; }` and sat above both, so
        everything from here down needed somebody to be MOVING. liveSince is
        only set when the server watches a character turn or walk. Somebody
        stuck at the character menu, on a load screen, or inside a race menu
        that will not close is by definition not moving, so the continue fired
        on every tick for ever and the two rescues below never ran once.

        MEASURED IN THE LIVE SERVER LOG, 21 September, four sessions and the
        same shape every time:

          13:35:33  chargen ff000056 profile 933578397 is new, in the hall
          13:35:33  chargen ff000056 -> appearance (already in the hall)
          13:41:55  disconnect 2

        Six minutes and twenty two seconds, nothing in between. The five minute
        give up should have fired at 13:40:33 and could not. A playtester
        described the same evening from the other side: "when I choose to make
        a new character it instead chooses to stay inside of the menu rather
        then taking me to the character creation".

        The comment over the nudge says "Nobody is ever left standing in the
        hall with nothing to do", and the guard was what made that untrue.

        WHY IT WAS THERE, because it is not wrong about everything. The NUDGE
        opens a race menu, and opening a second one while the first is still up
        took the game down with an access violation in a head part call. That
        is real, and liveSince is a decent proxy for 'no menu is open right
        now', so the nudge keeps the guard.

        THE GIVE UP OPENS NOTHING. It writes the state and moves on to the
        choices. There is no second menu to collide with and no reason it ever
        needed to know whether somebody was walking about. It is the only way
        out for a character who cannot move, which is precisely the character
        it exists for, so it runs on the clock like it says it does.

        AND THE NUDGE IS UNREACHABLE TODAY, which is worth writing down rather
        than leaving somebody to assume it is doing something. It needs
        liveSince, and the branch a dozen lines above ends the stage the moment
        onFeetFor passes MOVED_ENOUGH_MS, which is 500. So anybody with
        liveSince set has been moved on half a second in, 119 and a half seconds
        before the nudge could fire, and anybody without it is guarded out here.
        There is no path to it at all.

        It is kept rather than deleted because it is the safety net if
        MOVED_ENOUGH_MS is ever raised past NUDGE_MS, and because a race menu
        that never opened is still a real failure somebody may want to rescue
        differently later. What it must not do is read as a rescue that is
        running, because it is not.
      */
      if (!state.nudged && waited > NUDGE_MS && state.liveSince) {
        state.nudged = now;
        setChargen(actorId, state);
        try {
          mp.setRaceMenuOpen(actorId, true);
          log('chargen', actorId.toString(16),
              'walking around with no face after', Math.round(waited / 1000) +
              's, so the race menu never opened. Asking for it.');
        } catch (e) {
          log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), 'could not open the race menu:', e);
        }
        continue;
      }

      if (waited > GIVEUP_MS) {
        // Not while the plugin says the race menu is up: that person is making
        // a face, not stuck. See FACE_SEEN_MS, and FACE_CAP_MS for the one
        // exception. Said once, when the hold starts, and not every tick.
        const menuUp = !!state.faceSeenAt && now - state.faceSeenAt < FACE_SEEN_MS;
        const menuFor = state.faceOpenAt ? now - state.faceOpenAt : 0;
        if (menuUp && menuFor < FACE_CAP_MS) {
          if (!state.faceHeld) {
            state.faceHeld = now;
            setChargen(actorId, state);
            log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), 'no face after',
                Math.round(waited / 1000) + 's, but the race menu is still up,',
                'so the give up waits for it, for at most',
                Math.round(FACE_CAP_MS / 60000), 'minutes');
          }
          continue;
        }
        if (menuUp) {
          log('chargen', actorId.toString(16), 'the race menu has been up for',
              Math.round(menuFor / 60000), 'minutes, going on without it');
        }
        // Still no face. Go on without one rather than leave them standing
        // here: a character who never saw the race menu can change their face
        // later, a character who cannot leave the room can do nothing at all.
        log('chargen', actorId.toString(16),
            'appearance never arrived after', Math.round(waited / 1000) + 's,',
            'going on to the choices anyway');
        beginChoosing(actorId, state, 'no face after ' + Math.round(waited / 1000) + 's');
      }
    }
  }

  /*
    The starting purse, last, and the lastness is the point.

    It is here rather than in a loop of its own because this is the one pass
    that already walks everybody online. It costs a property read per person
    per tick and answers no on all but the first, and the reasoning for the
    forty is written out over purse() in the spoils block.

    IT USED TO RUN ABOVE THE LOOP AND THAT BROKE CREATION FOR EVERYBODY. The
    decision a hundred lines up asks whether somebody has played by looking in
    their pack. Handing a brand new character forty gold first put a thing in
    that pack, so they were read as a character who had already finished, had
    creation skipped and had their race menu closed, all on the first tick they
    were ever seen. Nothing in the log said anything was wrong; it said they
    already had a character, which is what it was written to say.

    So it goes after every decision that reads a pack. The count above also
    ignores gold now, so this being in the wrong place again would not be
    enough on its own to break it a second time.

    It stays outside the chargen state machine on purpose: a character who
    finished creation months ago still needs it, because the world they are
    standing in no longer has a coin in it.
  */
  const spoilsBlock = globalThis.__thornswoodSpoils;
  if (spoilsBlock && spoilsBlock.purse) {
    for (const who of online) {
      try { spoilsBlock.purse(who); } catch (e) { /* said in the spoils log */ }
    }
  }
}

function startChargenTimer() {
  if (!CHARGEN_ENABLED) {
    log('character creation is Skyrim Unbound\'s. The server is staying out of it.');
    return;
  }

  // Hot reload runs this file again in a fresh module, but the Node process and
  // its timers survive. Without this the ticks stack up one per save.
  const g = globalThis;
  if (g.__thornswoodChargenTimer) {
    clearInterval(g.__thornswoodChargenTimer);
  }
  g.__thornswoodChargenTimer = setInterval(guard('chargenTick', chargenTick),
                                           CHARGEN_TICK_MS);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// event handlers
//
// Every event a client can raise, and what this file answers to each, is
// listed once, over mp.onTakeItem beside mp.onPutItem further down.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

ensureProperties();
startChargenTimer();

/**
 * Set the arrival point at the moment of death, while we still know where the
 * body fell. The engine reads spawnPoint when the respawn timer expires.
 *
 * And, for an animal the hunt table answers for, set how long the body lies
 * there before the engine stands it back up. That half runs before the people
 * only test below, because it is the half that is about everything which is not
 * a person.
 */
mp.onDeath = guard('onDeath', function (actorId, killerId) {
  /*
    HOW LONG THE BODY LIES THERE, FOR AN ANIMAL A HUNTER CAN WORK.

    Read out of the fork on 20 September rather than assumed. Every actor that
    dies is stood back up: DeathEvent::OnFireSuccess calls
    MpActor::RespawnWithDelay, which sets a timer of ChangeForm().spawnDelay, and
    spawnDelay is 25.0f unless something sets it (MpChangeForms.h:109). Nothing
    here ever set it, so a carcass was pressable for twenty five seconds and a
    hunter who finished the fight and walked over found a live animal and got
    silence. The number this writes is HUNT_TUNING.bodySeconds and the case for
    it is written over that block.

    THE ORDER IS WHAT MAKES THIS WORK, and it is the engine's rather than
    anything this file arranges: GameModeEvent::Fire runs every listener, which
    is this function, and only then calls OnFireSuccess, which is the line that
    starts the respawn timer. GetRespawnTime is read inside that timer's setup,
    so a spawnDelay written here is the one that death uses. Written on every
    death rather than once, because it costs one property write and a table
    rebuild that adds an animal must not need a hand edit anywhere else.

    Animals in the table and nothing else. A bandit, a guard, a draugr and a
    dragon keep the engine's twenty five seconds, because no hunter has a verb
    for any of them and nothing else on this server reads spawnDelay.

    Wrapped, and a throw is not a failure worth a line in the log: baseDesc
    throws for a reference the world does not have any more, and spawnDelay
    throws for anything that is not an MpActor, which a death event should never
    hand us but is not this function's to prove.
  */
  try {
    const dying = mp.get(actorId, 'baseDesc');
    if (isCarcass(dying)) {
      mp.set(actorId, 'spawnDelay', HUNT_TUNING.bodySeconds);
    }
  } catch (e) {
    /* not a reference this server can read any more, so there is no body to
       leave lying and nothing to say about it */
  }

  /*
    AND THE WARRIOR'S WORK IS THE FIGHT.

    THE CIRCLE. A Warrior holds bulwark or berserker. Neither owns a recipe,
    because a warrior does not make things, and until now nothing anywhere
    credited either of them: not the recipe gate, not a station, not a verb.
    creditWork is the only road to addWork and addWork is the only road to
    rankUp, so a Warrior could play for a month and stay a novice for ever
    while a miner two feet away climbed. Audit-Systems calls it "OWNS NO
    RECIPE AT ALL, so somebody can pick it and make nothing", which is the
    symptom; the fault is that there was no way for them to climb at all.

    THIS IS THE SAME SHAPE THE MAGE HAD and the same answer. The note over
    treePointsPerRank says it: nothing here counts a spell being cast, and a
    mage was stuck holding two novice spells for good until two other things
    the server sees were made to count. The
    server does see a death, with its killer, and that is the warrior's.

    THE RULES, and each one is there to stop something.
      A PERSON KILLED BY A PERSON COUNTS FOR NOTHING. The dead actor is
      checked for a profileId below and the credit is above that check on
      purpose: only an NPC, an animal or a draugr pays. Paying for a kill
      between people is a bounty on the people this server is for.
      THE KILLER HAS TO BE A PERSON, for the same reason a bandit killing a
      chicken is not work.
      NOT YOURSELF. killerId equal to actorId is a fall or a fire, not a
      fight.
      ONLY A WARRIOR. Every other profession has a verb of its own and
      crediting them here as well would make killing the fastest way to be a
      tailor.

    It goes through creditWork like every other verb, so the tiredness drain
    and the party bonus apply and every ceiling in the rulebook still holds.
  */
  try {
    var kid = Number(killerId) || 0;
    if (kid && kid !== actorId) {
      var deadIsPerson = 0;
      try { deadIsPerson = Number(mp.get(actorId, 'profileId')) || 0; } catch (e) { deadIsPerson = 0; }
      var killerIsPerson = 0;
      try { killerIsPerson = Number(mp.get(kid, 'profileId')) || 0; } catch (e) { killerIsPerson = 0; }
      if (!deadIsPerson && killerIsPerson) {
        var ch = globalThis.__thornswoodCharacter;
        var st = (ch && ch.stateOf) ? ch.stateOf(kid) : null;
        if (st && st.prof === 'warrior' && st.half) {
          var raised = creditWork(kid, st.half);
          if (raised) {
            notify(kid, raised);
            say(kid, raised);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  } catch (e) {
    log('could not count a kill for', (killerId || 0).toString(16) + ':', e);
  }

  /*
    People only.

    This fires for every MpActor the server owns, which is every bandit, wolf
    and chicken anybody kills. Each one was having a temple spawnPoint written
    onto it, which is meaningless at best and, if SkyMP ever honours it on an
    NPC respawn, puts dead bandits back on their feet inside temples.

    A profileId is what tells a person from the rest of Skyrim. Nothing else
    on the server has one.
  */
  let pid = 0;
  try { pid = Number(mp.get(actorId, 'profileId')) || 0; } catch (e) { pid = 0; }
  if (!pid) { return; }

  const site = {
    desc: mp.get(actorId, 'worldOrCellDesc'),
    pos: mp.get(actorId, 'pos'),
  };
  const arrival = resolveArrival(site);
  if (!arrival) {
    log('onDeath', actorId, 'no arrival resolved for', site.desc, '- leaving default spawn point');
    return;
  }
  mp.set(actorId, 'spawnPoint', {
    cellOrWorldDesc: arrival.desc,
    pos: arrival.pos,
    rot: arrival.rot,
  });
  log('death', actorId, 'in', site.desc,
      '-> ' + arrival.cell + ' (' + arrival.slot + ')',
      'hold=' + arrival.hold + '(' + arrival.via + ')',
      'ground=' + arrival.reason,
      killerId ? 'killer=' + killerId : '');
});

mp.onRespawn = guard('onRespawn', function (actorId) {
  const sp = mp.get(actorId, 'spawnPoint');
  log('respawn', actorId, 'at', sp && sp.cellOrWorldDesc);
});

// No book moves anybody's rank. Patrick, 21 September: rank comes from working
// the trade and from nothing else. There is no book event here any more and the
// manuals are gone from the plugin. A book in this world is a book.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// scripted activators
//
// A shrine, a lever, a chain, a notice board and a nirnroot are the same kind
// of record: ACTI, an activator whose whole behaviour lives in an attached
// Papyrus script. On this server not one of those scripts runs, for two
// reasons that stack.
//
//   1. The server has no scripts. Its script storage is data/scripts plus any
//      archive named in the "archives" setting, and data/scripts holds exactly
//      one file: ActiveMagicEffect.pex. "archives" is not set at all, so
//      Skyrim's own script archive is never opened. Nothing vanilla is
//      attached to anything, anywhere.
//
//   2. Even with scripts present, the client is told not to run them. SkyMP
//      answers an activator with a bare OpenContainer message and the client
//      replies to that with refr.activate(player, true). The second argument
//      is abDefaultProcessingOnly: default behaviour only, no OnActivate. So
//      the script does not fire locally either.
//
// Which is why pressing E on a shrine does nothing at all, and why nirnroot
// cannot be picked: nirnroot is ACTI 0xE1FB2, not a FLOR, so it never reaches
// the branch of the server that hands out a harvested plant.
//
// The two below are done by hand because both effects are server side and
// therefore reliable: an ingredient into the pack, which is an ordinary
// inventory write, and an ability onto the character, which Actor.AddSpell
// records server side and re-sends on every spawn. Levers, chains and notice
// boards are not done here. They need the real script and there is no way to
// fake what they do; the way back for those is putting Skyrim's .pex files in
// the server's data/scripts.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/*
  EVERY PLANT IN THE LOAD ORDER, AND IT IS GENERATED.

  Everything between the declaration below and the line that closes the block
  is written by the harvests pass in tools\Build-HarvestTable.ps1, out of the
  FLOR and TREE records of the 101 plugins and 5 masters this build enables:
  147 plants, counted on 20 September, the same table as the harvests key of
  tools\harvests.json. DO NOT HAND EDIT IT. The next run of that script
  replaces every line between them and takes any correction with it. Same
  arrangement as CROPS_BY_BASE below and SPELL_IDS further down.

  It is seeded empty and the script fills it. A row says:

    produce  the item the record hands over when the plant is picked.
    name     what a person is told they picked.
    rank     THE GATHERER'S LADDER, and unlike the crops this is the one that
             gates. harvests.json ranks by the gold value of the produce
             against the thresholds at the top of it, which is a fair reading
             of what a plant is worth to pick: measured on 20 September the 147
             rows come out 57 novice, 61 advanced, 24 expert and 5 master. That
             is the opposite of what the same arithmetic did to the crops, where
             ten of eleven landed on one rung, which is why the crops carry a
             hand written rule beside them and the plants do not need one.

  THE TABLE WAS 181 ROWS AND THE FIRST RUNG OF IT WAS EMPTY IN THE OPEN WORLD.
  Measured on 20 September, the same day the table went in: 50 rows ranked
  novice, and 33 of them were the HearthFires planter sacks, which are a MISC
  the records price at nothing and a Papyrus script opens into three of the real
  ingredient, so they gated nothing and handed over a sack that this server
  cannot open. One was the clam at Oar's Rest and one the tree in the Ancestor
  Glade. The 15 that were left were every one of them a white cap, an imp stool
  or a namira's rot in a cave. So a new Gatherer was refused every flower in
  Skyrim, and with workPerRank.advanced at 20 as it was then, the only road onto
  the second rung was twenty cave mushrooms. The pass now leaves the sacks out and reads the
  novice band as produce worth up to 1 gold, which is lavender, thistle, creep
  cluster, juniper berries, jazbay grapes, hanging moss and the rest of them:
  47 wild bases on the first rung instead of 15. The three numbers that did
  that are RANK_MAX_VALUE, PRODUCE_A_SCRIPT_MUST_OPEN and OUR_STATIONS, all in
  the knobs at the top of the script, each with what was measured beside it.

  AND A PLANT WHOSE PRODUCE IS FOOD IS NEVER A ROW HERE EITHER, which is the
  change of 21 September and the largest one this table has had. Patrick: 'The
  farmer grows the crops? Those crops should be used to make food, why is a
  gatherer giving them all the food ingredients when they gather herbs and
  things.' Measured that day over the 131 rows this table then had, 106 gave an
  alchemy reagent, which is the gatherer's work as classes.md describes it, and
  24 gave food. Counted the same day over tools\recipes.json, the gatherer
  supplied the Cook 225 of that profession's inputs and the farmer supplied the
  Cook nothing at all.

  So the pass now sorts on the produce: a plant whose produce is an alchemy
  reagent is the gatherer's and a plant whose produce is food is not, food being
  bit 0x2 of an ALCH record's ENIT flag word, which tools\Build-RecipeTable.ps1
  proves against the five masters and already gates the whole Cook and Alchemist
  split on. Of the 24, thirteen were grown crops and went to the crops map,
  seven were hanging game and went to the game map above, two were props that
  are neither a plant nor an animal and went nowhere, and two were the Soul Husk
  and stayed here on purpose, because it grows wild in the Soul Cairn and is an
  input to no recipe in this build. All of that is argued out over the food
  knobs in the script, and the run prints the four counts every time.

  A BASE THIS SERVER PLACES ITSELF IS NEVER A ROW HERE. The pass reads the whole
  load order and Thornswood-Content.esp is in it, so the beehive this server put
  in the world was swept up as a wild plant worth 5 gold and gated behind an
  Advanced Gatherer, which nobody decided and which blocked the engine's own
  handover for everybody else as well. The pass reads the same stations file the
  STATIONS line at the top of this file is generated from and leaves those bases
  out, and plantAt below refuses one a second time in case a future station
  reaches this table another way.

  NOTHING ABOVE MAY SPELL THE CLOSING MARKER OUT, for the reason written out at
  length over the crops block below: the install takes the first match of it in
  the whole file and refuses when that lands above the declaration, so one
  quotation in a comment turns the pass into a no-op that reports no fault.
*/
// ==== HARVESTS_BY_BASE, generated ====
